window.MIBEM_INDEX = [{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"Introduction to computational materials science","u":"computational/introduction.html","x":"Topics / Foundations Topic 1 Introduction to computational materials science Computational materials science uses physical models and numerical methods to predict, explain and design the structure, processing and properties of materials. No single model covers every scale, so the field is a toolbox of methods, each valid over a limited range of length, time and chemistry. Reading time about 20 min Builds on Nothing: this is the place to start Leads to 2. Crystals and cells 3. Statistical mechanics Category Foundations After reading this page you should be able to: Place any simulation method on a length–time map and state what it does and does not resolve. Describe the physical basis, inputs, outputs and main approximations of each major method family. Explain how methods are linked into multiscale workflows (ICME). Distinguish verification, validation and uncertainty quantification, and apply basic convergence checks. Pick a sensible first method for a given materials question, and find software and data to start with."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"1. Scope and purpose","u":"computational/introduction.html#scope","x":"The central problem of materials science is the processing–structure–properties–performance chain: how a material is made determines its structure at every scale, structure determines properties, and properties determine how it performs in service. Computation can be applied to any link in this chain. In the forward direction it predicts properties from a given structure or composition. In the inverse direction it searches for compositions and processes that deliver a target property. What simulation adds to experiment Access to what cannot be measured: atomic-scale mechanisms, short-lived intermediates, internal fields during deformation, temperature or composition in places no probe can reach. Speed and cost: thousands of virtual compositions can be screened before a single melt is made. Isolation of variables: a simulation can change one parameter, such as one solute or one boundary type, while holding everything else fixed, which real samples rarely allow. Interpretation: models give a mechanism that explains why a measured trend occurs, which is what allows extrapolation beyond tested conditions. What simulation cannot do Every model contains approximations and needs inputs, and the output is no better than either. Models are usually most trustworthy for trends and for ranking candidates, and least trustworthy for absolute numbers on complex real material, which has defects, impurities, residual stress and a processing history the model may not represent. A prediction is a hypothesis to be tested, not a measurement. Integrated computational materials engineering ICME is the practice of linking models across scales, together with experiments and databases, to shorten the path from composition and process to component performance. The US Materials Genome Initiative (launched 2011) pushed the related idea of combining computation, experiment and data to cut the time needed to bring new materials to use."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"2. Length and time scales","u":"computational/introduction.html#scales","x":"Material behaviour arises from structure at many scales at once: electrons bond atoms, atoms form defects, defects interact to form microstructure, and microstructure controls the bulk response. Cost grows steeply as a model resolves finer detail, so a method covers only a window of scales. The table summarises the windows; values are order-of-magnitude and depend on hardware and implementation. Level Length Time Representative methods Typical outputs Electronic 0.1–2 nm; up to a few hundred atoms routinely, thousands with effort Static, or picoseconds for ab initio molecular dynamics Density functional theory, quantum chemistry Energies, forces, elastic constants, bonding, defect formation energies, band structure Atomistic 1 nm to about 100 nm; millions to billions of atoms on large machines Picoseconds to microseconds Molecular dynamics, Metropolis Monte Carlo Diffusion coefficients, interface energies, dislocation and crack behaviour, thermal properties Thermodynamic No spatial scale (bulk, equilibrium) Equilibrium, or simple kinetics CALPHAD Phase diagrams, phase fractions and compositions, driving forces, transformation temperatures Mesoscale 10 nm to about 1 mm Microseconds to hours Phase-field, kinetic Monte Carlo, dislocation dynamics, crystal plasticity, cellular automata Microstructure evolution, grain and phase morphology, texture, grain-level stress and strain Continuum Micrometres to metres Seconds to years Finite element, finite volume, computational fluid dynamics Temperature, flow, stress, strain, distortion, residual stress, component response Data-driven Any Any Regression, neural networks, Gaussian processes Fast surrogate predictions, screening, inverse design Length windows of the main methods on a logarithmic axis, with the time each can reach. The windows overlap, which is what makes linking them possible. Two gaps matter in practice. First, atomistic time scales (up to microseconds) are many orders of magnitude shorter than the time scales of diffusion-controlled processing (seconds to hours). Second, atomistic strain rates are typically 10 7 s −1 or higher, against about 10 −3 s −1 in a tensile test. Bridging these gaps is a main reason for coarse-grained, mesoscale and accelerated-dynamics methods."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.1 Density functional theory (DFT)","u":"computational/introduction.html#dft","x":"Basis. The Hohenberg–Kohn theorems (1964) show that the ground-state energy of an interacting electron system is a unique functional of the electron density, and the Kohn–Sham approach (1965) replaces the many-body problem by non-interacting electrons moving in an effective potential: [ −½∇² + v ext (r) + v H (r) + v xc (r) ] ψ i (r) = ε i ψ i (r), n(r) = Σ i |ψ i (r)|² Here v ext is the nuclear potential, v H the classical electron–electron (Hartree) term, and v xc the exchange–correlation potential, which contains all the remaining many-body physics and is the one quantity that must be approximated. The equations are solved self-consistently because n (r) enters the potential that determines it. Approximations to choose. The local density approximation (LDA) uses the exchange–correlation energy of a uniform electron gas; generalised gradient approximations (GGA, for example PBE) add dependence on the density gradient; meta-GGAs (for example SCAN) add kinetic-energy density; hybrid functionals (for example HSE06) mix in part of exact exchange at higher cost. Other practical choices are the pseudopotential or PAW dataset, the plane-wave cutoff energy, the k -point mesh for Brillouin-zone sampling, smearing for metals, spin polarisation, and corrections such as DFT+U for localised d and f states or van der Waals terms for layered and molecular materials. Outputs. Total energies (hence formation energies, defect energies, surface and interface energies, migration barriers by nudged elastic band), forces and stresses (hence relaxed structures, elastic constants, phonons), electronic density of states and band structure, magnetic moments. Cost and limits. Conventional Kohn–Sham DFT scales roughly with the cube of the system size, which limits cells to hundreds or a few thousand atoms. Standard GGA tends to underestimate band gaps of semiconductors and insulators, and treats strongly correlated systems poorly. Results are normally at 0 K and for perfect periodic cells, so finite-temperature and long-time behaviour need additional steps. Magnetism can decide the answer: LDA famously predicts the wrong ground-state structure of iron, which GGA corrects."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.2 Molecular dynamics (MD)","u":"computational/introduction.html#md","x":"Basis. Atoms are treated as classical particles that move according to Newton’s equations under forces derived from an interatomic potential U : m i d²r i /dt² = F i = −∇ i U(r 1 , …, r N ) The equations are integrated in small time steps (typically about 1 fs, shorter if light atoms such as hydrogen are involved; the step must resolve the fastest vibration). Statistical-mechanical ensembles are imposed with thermostats and barostats: NVE (constant energy), NVT (constant temperature) and NPT (constant temperature and pressure). Properties are time or ensemble averages over the trajectory. Interatomic potentials are the decisive input. Lennard–Jones and Morse are simple pair potentials. The embedded-atom method (EAM, Daw and Baskes, 1984) and modified EAM include an environment-dependent term and are standard for metals. Stillinger–Weber and Tersoff describe directional covalent bonding (silicon, carbon). ReaxFF allows bond breaking and forming. A growing class of machine-learned potentials (for example Gaussian approximation, moment tensor and neural-network potentials) is fitted to DFT data and approaches DFT accuracy at far lower cost, within the chemical and structural range they were trained on. Outputs. Diffusion coefficients from mean-squared displacement, thermal expansion, melting point, free-energy differences, elastic and thermal properties, dislocation and crack mechanisms, grain boundary structure and energy, radiation damage cascades. Limits. The result is only as good as the potential, which may be wrong outside the data it was fitted to. Time scales stop at microseconds and strain rates are extreme. Electrons are absent, so electronic and magnetic effects are missing unless built into the potential."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.3 Monte Carlo methods","u":"computational/introduction.html#mc","x":"Monte Carlo (MC) samples configurations randomly instead of following physical trajectories. In Metropolis MC (1953), a trial change from state a to state b is accepted with probability P(a→b) = min[ 1, exp( −(E b − E a ) / k B T ) ] so that the configurations visited follow the Boltzmann distribution. Because moves need not be physical, MC can equilibrate quantities that MD reaches too slowly, such as chemical ordering, segregation of solutes to interfaces, or composition in multicomponent alloys (swap moves). Kinetic Monte Carlo (kMC) instead picks events, such as an atom hopping into a vacancy, with rates from transition-state theory, and advances a physically meaningful clock; it reaches seconds or longer but needs a catalogue of possible events and their barriers, often taken from DFT. Lattice models such as the Ising model and cluster expansions, fitted to DFT energies, provide the energy function for many alloy MC studies."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.4 CALPHAD","u":"computational/introduction.html#calphad","x":"Basis. The CALPHAD (CALculation of PHAse Diagrams) method models the molar Gibbs energy of every phase as a function of temperature, pressure and composition, and finds equilibrium by minimising the total Gibbs energy of the system. A typical solution-phase model has the form G m = G ref + G ideal + G excess + G mag , G ideal = RT Σ x i ln x i where the excess term is commonly written as a Redlich–Kister polynomial in composition, and phases with distinct sublattices (carbides, intermetallics, interstitial solutions) use the compound energy formalism. Model parameters are fitted simultaneously to phase-diagram data, thermochemical measurements and first-principles results, and collected into thermodynamic databases (often distributed as .tdb files). Binary and ternary descriptions are extrapolated to multicomponent systems. Outputs. Phase diagrams and isopleths, equilibrium phase amounts and compositions, solidus and liquidus, solvus lines, transformation temperatures, activities, driving forces for precipitation, and (with the Scheil–Gulliver model) solidification paths under limited diffusion. Limits. It is a bulk equilibrium method with no geometry, no interfaces and no time unless coupled to a kinetic model. Accuracy depends on how well the database covers the composition and temperature region of interest, which is a common source of error for unusual compositions. Coupling with diffusion mobility databases allows simulation of diffusion-controlled transformations in one dimension and supplies the thermodynamic input to phase-field models."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.5 Phase-field modelling","u":"computational/introduction.html#phasefield","x":"Basis. Interfaces are represented as diffuse transition layers, so the interface need not be tracked explicitly. One or more continuous order parameters φ( r , t ) distinguish phases or grains, and the free energy of the whole microstructure is a functional F = ∫ [ f(φ, c, T) + ½ κ φ |∇φ|² + ½ κ c |∇c|² + f el ] dV Evolution follows by relaxation toward lower free energy. For a non-conserved order parameter (grain orientation, phase identity) this is the Allen–Cahn equation, ∂φ/∂ t = − L δ F /δφ. For a conserved quantity such as composition it is the Cahn–Hilliard equation, ∂ c /∂ t = ∇·( M ∇δ F /δ c ). Elastic, magnetic and electrostatic energies can be added to the functional. Typical applications. Dendritic and cellular solidification, grain growth, spinodal decomposition and precipitation, martensitic and diffusional solid-state transformations, ferroelectric domain evolution, fracture and corrosion fronts. Practical points. The numerical grid must resolve the interface width, which makes large domains and long times expensive; thin-interface formulations relax this. Inputs are free-energy densities (often from CALPHAD), mobilities, interface energies and anisotropies, many of which are poorly known and must be taken from atomistic work or calibrated."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.6 Dislocation dynamics and crystal plasticity","u":"computational/introduction.html#cp","x":"Discrete dislocation dynamics (DDD) follows individual dislocation lines, moving each under the Peach–Koehler force from applied stress and from other dislocations, with rules for reactions such as junction formation and cross-slip. It reaches micrometre-scale volumes and addresses hardening and size effects. Crystal plasticity treats each grain as a single crystal that deforms by slip on defined systems. Slip on system α begins when the resolved shear stress, τ α = m α ·σ· n α (Schmid’s law), reaches a critical value, and the shear rate follows a flow rule with hardening laws. Implemented in a finite-element or spectral solver (crystal plasticity finite element method, CPFEM; spectral solvers such as DAMASK), it predicts texture evolution, anisotropy, grain-level strain partitioning and the response of polycrystals and multiphase aggregates, using a representative volume element (RVE) built from measured or synthetic microstructure."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.7 Continuum methods","u":"computational/introduction.html#continuum","x":"Continuum models treat the material as a smooth medium and solve conservation laws as partial differential equations: heat conduction, mass and momentum balance (fluid flow), species transport, and mechanical equilibrium with a constitutive law linking stress to strain and strain rate. The finite element method (FEM) divides the domain into elements and solves a weak form of the equations; the finite volume method is common in computational fluid dynamics (CFD). They are used for casting and welding thermal fields, forging, rolling and forming, heat treatment distortion, residual stress, fatigue and fracture assessment, and component-level design. Accuracy rests on the constitutive law and on boundary conditions, such as heat-transfer coefficients and friction, which are often the dominant uncertainty. Mesh convergence and time-step control are essential."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.8 Machine learning","u":"computational/introduction.html#ml","x":"Machine learning (ML) fits flexible models to data instead of deriving them from physics. In materials work it appears in four roles: (i) property prediction from composition or structure descriptors, (ii) interatomic potentials fitted to DFT, (iii) surrogate models that replace expensive simulations, and (iv) inverse design and active learning, where the model proposes the next experiment or calculation (for example by Bayesian optimisation). Key issues are data quantity and quality (materials datasets are small and biased toward successful experiments), the choice of descriptors or learned representations, the reliability of predictions outside the training domain (extrapolation), uncertainty estimates, and interpretability. Proper cross-validation matters: random splits of related compounds leak information, and splitting by composition family or by time is a stricter and more honest test. Physics-informed models, which embed symmetries or known constraints, generally need less data."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"4. Linking scales","u":"computational/introduction.html#multiscale","x":"Multiscale modelling connects the methods above. Two coupling strategies are common. Hierarchical (sequential, parameter passing): a finer model computes a quantity that is passed as a parameter to a coarser model. Example: DFT gives defect and interface energies; MD or kMC gives diffusivities and mobilities; CALPHAD supplies free energies; a phase-field model then uses all of these to predict microstructure; the microstructure feeds a crystal-plasticity or continuum model for properties. Concurrent: different regions or levels run together and exchange information during the run, as in quantum-mechanics/molecular-mechanics (QM/MM) or atomistic/continuum coupling around a crack tip. These are harder to set up and less common. Information also flows downward: a continuum thermal model provides the temperature history that a microstructure model needs. Along every interface of the chain, errors propagate, so each link should be validated on its own before the chain is trusted."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"5. A typical workflow","u":"computational/introduction.html#workflow","x":"Define the question in measurable terms: which property, which condition, what accuracy is needed. Select the model from the smallest feature and longest time that matter (section 7). Prepare inputs: structure or geometry, composition, potential or database, boundary and initial conditions, material parameters. Test convergence of numerical parameters before production runs (section 10). Run on a workstation or cluster; script it so that it is repeatable. Post-process and visualise; compute uncertainties, not only a single number. Validate against independent experiment or a higher-level calculation (section 6). Document versions, input files and random seeds so the work can be reproduced."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"6. Verification, validation and uncertainty","u":"computational/introduction.html#vv","x":"These three terms are often confused. Verification Are the equations solved correctly? Checks against analytical solutions, benchmark problems, mesh and time-step refinement, and code tests. Validation Are the right equations being solved? Comparison of predictions with experiments that were not used to fit the model. Uncertainty quantification How confident should one be in the number? Propagates uncertainty in inputs, parameters and numerical error to the output, and distinguishes systematic error (a poor model or potential) from statistical error (finite sampling). Typical sources of error include the exchange–correlation functional in DFT, the potential in MD, database coverage in CALPHAD, the neglect of defects and impurities, finite-size and finite-time effects, discretisation error, and poorly known input parameters. A model that reproduces its own training data has been fitted, not validated."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"7. Choosing a method","u":"computational/introduction.html#choosing","x":"Start from the question, not the tool. Three questions usually narrow the choice quickly: What is the smallest feature that controls the answer? Bond energies and electronic effects point to DFT; defect cores and interfaces point to atomistics; grains and precipitates point to mesoscale; whole components point to continuum models. What is the longest time that matters? Microsecond limits rule out direct MD for diffusional processing; use kMC, phase-field or a continuum model. Is the answer an equilibrium state or a rate? Equilibrium is often settled by thermodynamics alone, which is cheap; rates need a kinetic model. Question Reasonable first method Which phases are stable at this composition and temperature? CALPHAD How strongly does a solute bind to a defect or interface? DFT What is the diffusion coefficient or boundary mobility of an element? MD, kMC or DFT-derived barriers; or experimental data How does a dislocation or crack behave in this lattice? MD (with a validated potential) How does the microstructure evolve during heat treatment or solidification? Phase-field, with CALPHAD input How does a polycrystal deform and develop texture? Crystal plasticity What temperature, stress and distortion arise in the process or part? FEM or CFD Which of thousands of candidates should be tried first? High-throughput DFT or machine learning Often the cheapest useful step is a thermodynamic calculation, because it reveals which phases can exist before heavier modelling is attempted."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"8. Software","u":"computational/introduction.html#software","x":"The list is a starting map, not an endorsement; availability and licences change. Category Open source Commercial Electronic structure Quantum ESPRESSO, ABINIT, GPAW, CP2K VASP, CASTEP, Gaussian Molecular dynamics LAMMPS, GROMACS, OpenMM Materials Studio, Schrödinger suite Thermodynamics and kinetics pycalphad, OpenCALPHAD Thermo-Calc and DICTRA, FactSage, Pandat, JMatPro Mesoscale MOOSE, OpenPhase, PRISMS-PF, DAMASK, MMSP MICRESS Continuum OpenFOAM, CalculiX, FEniCS, MOOSE Abaqus, Ansys, COMSOL, DEFORM, Simufact Workflow and analysis ASE, pymatgen, AiiDA, OVITO, VESTA, ParaView — Machine learning scikit-learn, PyTorch, JAX, matminer — Python is the usual glue language: scripting inputs, running jobs, parsing outputs and plotting. Large jobs run on high-performance computing (HPC) clusters through a scheduler such as Slurm, with parallelism by MPI, threads or GPUs."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"9. Databases and data","u":"computational/introduction.html#data","x":"Materials Project , AFLOW , OQMD and JARVIS : large collections of DFT-computed structures and properties. NOMAD : repository and archive for computational materials data from many codes. Crystallographic databases: Inorganic Crystal Structure Database (ICSD, licensed), Crystallography Open Database (COD, open), Pearson’s Crystal Data. Thermodynamic and phase-diagram data: CALPHAD databases (commercial and some open), the ASM Alloy Phase Diagram Database, and NIST resources. Interatomic potentials: the NIST Interatomic Potentials Repository and OpenKIM, which also provides tests for potentials. Computed values in public databases carry the approximations of their method (typically GGA), so treat them as consistent comparisons rather than exact numbers."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"10. Good practice","u":"computational/introduction.html#habits","x":"Converge before you compute. Vary the plane-wave cutoff and k -point mesh in DFT, the cell size and time step in MD, the grid spacing and time step in phase-field, and the mesh in FEM, until the quantity of interest stops changing within the accuracy you need. Check the size effects. Periodic cells that are too small interact with their own images; MD boxes must be large compared with the defect or correlation length. Equilibrate and sample enough. Discard the start-up transient; report averages with error bars from independent runs or block averaging. Mind units and conventions. Codes use different unit systems (for example LAMMPS “metal” units) and different sign and normalisation conventions. Start simple. Reproduce a known result, such as a lattice parameter, an elastic constant or a textbook phase boundary, before attempting a novel one. Keep it reproducible. Store inputs, versions and seeds under version control, and archive the data that supports a published figure. Key takeaways Methods are specialised by length and time scale; no single model covers every scale, so multiscale linking is the norm. Start from the question: the smallest feature that matters, the longest time that matters, and whether the answer is an equilibrium or a rate. A simulation is a prediction to be tested. Verification, validation and uncertainty quantification are three different checks. The cheapest useful step is often a thermodynamic (CALPHAD) calculation, before heavier modelling is attempted. Inputs and approximations bound accuracy: trends and rankings are usually more reliable than absolute numbers."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"11. Check your understanding","u":"computational/introduction.html#check","x":"Which method would you reach for first to estimate the vacancy formation energy of copper, and why? Density functional theory: the quantity is an energy difference between a small cell with and without the defect, which needs electronic accuracy rather than a long trajectory. If a validated embedded-atom potential exists, molecular dynamics or static relaxation with that potential gives a much cheaper first estimate that can be checked against a few DFT calculations. Why can plain molecular dynamics not follow the homogenisation of a segregated alloy? Homogenisation is diffusion over micrometres lasting seconds to hours, while molecular dynamics reaches tens of nanometres and nanoseconds to microseconds. A kinetic Monte Carlo, phase-field or continuum diffusion model covers the relevant scales, with diffusivities and thermodynamics supplied by finer-scale methods. What is the difference between verification and validation? Verification asks whether the equations are solved correctly (convergence tests, benchmarks, code checks). Validation asks whether the right equations and inputs are being used, by comparing predictions with independent experiments that were not used to fit the model."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"12. Glossary","u":"computational/introduction.html#glossary","x":"Ab initio “From first principles”: methods that use no fitted parameters for the material of interest, such as DFT. Ensemble The set of conditions held fixed in a statistical-mechanics simulation (NVE, NVT, NPT). Gibbs energy The thermodynamic potential that is minimised at equilibrium at constant temperature and pressure. Interatomic potential A function giving the energy of a set of atoms from their positions, used in classical simulations. Order parameter A field that distinguishes one phase or grain orientation from another in phase-field models. Pseudopotential An effective potential that replaces core electrons in DFT so that only valence electrons are treated explicitly. Representative volume element (RVE) A volume of microstructure large enough that its averaged response represents the bulk material. Surrogate model A fast approximate model, often machine-learned, fitted to the output of an expensive simulation. Transition-state theory A theory that gives the rate of a thermally activated event from the energy barrier between two states."},{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"13. References and further reading","u":"computational/introduction.html#refs","x":"Check each citation against the original before relying on it in your own work. Hohenberg, P. and Kohn, W., Inhomogeneous electron gas, Phys. Rev. 136, B864 (1964). Kohn, W. and Sham, L. J., Self-consistent equations including exchange and correlation effects, Phys. Rev. 140, A1133 (1965). Perdew, J. P., Burke, K. and Ernzerhof, M., Generalized gradient approximation made simple, Phys. Rev. Lett. 77, 3865 (1996). Metropolis, N. et al., Equation of state calculations by fast computing machines, J. Chem. Phys. 21, 1087 (1953). Daw, M. S. and Baskes, M. I., Embedded-atom method, Phys. Rev. B 29, 6443 (1984). Cahn, J. W. and Hilliard, J. E., Free energy of a nonuniform system. I. Interfacial free energy, J. Chem. Phys. 28, 258 (1958). Allen, S. M. and Cahn, J. W., A microscopic theory for antiphase boundary motion, Acta Metall. 27, 1085 (1979). Plimpton, S., Fast parallel algorithms for short-range molecular dynamics, J. Comput. Phys. 117, 1 (1995). Lukas, H. L., Fries, S. G. and Sundman, B., Computational Thermodynamics: The Calphad Method , Cambridge University Press (2007). Frenkel, D. and Smit, B., Understanding Molecular Simulation , Academic Press. Allen, M. P. and Tildesley, D. J., Computer Simulation of Liquids , Oxford University Press. Martin, R. M., Electronic Structure: Basic Theory and Practical Methods , Cambridge University Press. Raabe, D., Computational Materials Science: The Simulation of Materials, Microstructures and Properties , Wiley-VCH (1998). Jain, A. et al., Commentary: The Materials Project, APL Materials 1, 011002 (2013). Next topic Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries"},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html","x":"Topics / Foundations Topic 2 Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries Almost every atomistic simulation starts by writing down where the atoms are. For crystals, a handful of numbers describes an infinite solid; for defects, the same description is stretched into a finite, repeating box that must be chosen with care. Reading time about 12 min Builds on 1. Introduction Leads to 5. Density functional theory 6. Molecular dynamics Category Foundations After reading this page you should be able to: Describe a crystal as a lattice plus a basis and count atoms, neighbours and packing for common structures. Use Miller indices for directions and planes, and relate real and reciprocal lattices. Choose a supercell for a point defect, surface or interface, and estimate the defect concentration it implies. Apply the minimum-image convention and explain the size effects that periodic boundaries introduce."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"1. Lattice and basis","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#lattice","x":"An ideal crystal is invariant under translation by any vector of a Bravais lattice : R = n 1 a 1 + n 2 a 2 + n 3 a 3 , n i integers where a 1,2,3 are the primitive lattice vectors. The parallelepiped they span is the primitive cell : the smallest volume that tiles space by translation and contains exactly one lattice point. The crystal is the lattice decorated with a basis , a set of atoms at positions r j inside each cell, usually given as fractional coordinates ( x , y , z ) between 0 and 1 along the lattice vectors: position of atom j in cell n: r = R n + (x j a 1 + y j a 2 + z j a 3 ) A cell can be described by six numbers: three lengths a , b , c and three angles α, β, γ. Symmetry sorts all lattices into seven crystal systems (cubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic, hexagonal, trigonal, monoclinic, triclinic) and fourteen Bravais lattices once centring is included (primitive P, body-centred I, face-centred F, base-centred C). Adding the basis and its symmetry gives 230 space groups . Symmetry matters computationally: it reduces the number of distinct k -points in electronic-structure calculations and the number of independent elastic constants (3 for cubic, 5 for hexagonal, 21 for triclinic). Often a conventional cell larger than the primitive cell is used because it shows the symmetry more clearly. The face-centred cubic conventional cell holds 4 lattice points, the body-centred cubic one holds 2."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"2. Common structures","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#structures","x":"Structure Lattice Atoms per conventional cell Nearest neighbours Packing fraction Examples Face-centred cubic (fcc) cubic F 4 12 0.74 Al, Cu, Ni, Au, austenite (γ-Fe) Body-centred cubic (bcc) cubic I 2 8 (6 more at 15% larger distance) 0.68 α-Fe (ferrite), W, Cr, Mo Hexagonal close-packed (hcp) hexagonal P, 2-atom basis 2 (in the hexagonal cell) 12 0.74 at ideal c/a = √(8/3) ≈ 1.633 Mg, Ti, Zn, Co Diamond cubic fcc, 2-atom basis 8 4 0.34 Si, Ge, C (diamond) Rock salt (B1) fcc, 2-atom basis 8 (4 cation + 4 anion) 6 (unlike ions) depends on radii NaCl, MgO, TiC Perovskite (ABO 3 ) simple cubic (ideal) 5 B: 6 O; A: 12 O — SrTiO 3 , BaTiO 3 Close packing arises from stacking triangular layers. The sequence ABAB… gives hcp and ABCABC… gives fcc, which explains why the two structures have the same density and coordination but different symmetry, and why stacking faults in fcc metals locally look like hcp. Bcc is less densely packed and has no single close-packed slip plane; the mobility of its screw dislocations depends strongly on temperature, which is the origin of the ductile-to-brittle transition that bcc metals show and fcc metals do not. Lattice parameters at room temperature are the first numbers to check against any model: Cu fcc a ≈ 3.61 Å, Al fcc 4.05 Å, α-Fe bcc 2.87 Å, Si diamond 5.43 Å. If a calculation or potential reproduces these to within a percent or two, the basic bonding description is plausible; if not, nothing downstream can be trusted."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"3. Directions and planes","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#indices","x":"Directions are written [ uvw ]: the smallest integers proportional to the components of the vector in lattice coordinates, with negative values shown by a bar. A family of equivalent directions is written ⟨ uvw ⟩. Planes use Miller indices ( hkl ): take the intercepts of the plane with the axes in units of the lattice parameters, invert them, and clear fractions. A family of equivalent planes is written { hkl }. In cubic crystals, [ hkl ] is perpendicular to ( hkl ), but this is not true in general for other systems. The spacing of ( hkl ) planes in a cubic crystal is d hkl = a / √(h² + k² + l²) which is used directly in diffraction (Bragg’s law, n λ = 2 d sinθ) and to compare simulated structures with X-ray or electron diffraction. Close-packed planes and directions control plasticity: slip occurs most readily on {111}⟨110⟩ in fcc and on {110}, {112} and {123} planes along ⟨111⟩ in bcc. Surface energy also depends on orientation, with close-packed surfaces such as fcc (111) typically lowest. Hexagonal crystals use four-index Miller–Bravais notation ( hkil ) with i = −( h + k ), which makes equivalent planes look alike."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"4. Reciprocal lattice","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#reciprocal","x":"Waves in a periodic solid, whether electrons, phonons or X-rays, are best described in reciprocal space . The reciprocal lattice vectors satisfy a i · b j = 2π δ ij , i.e. b 1 = 2π (a 2 × a 3 ) / [a 1 · (a 2 × a 3 )] and cyclic permutations The Wigner–Seitz cell of the reciprocal lattice is the first Brillouin zone . A key rule: the larger the real-space cell, the smaller its Brillouin zone, so a supercell needs fewer k -points. The reciprocal lattice vector G hkl = h b 1 + k b 2 + l b 3 is perpendicular to the ( hkl ) planes and has length 2π/ d hkl , which links this section to Section 3."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"5. Defects","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#defects","x":"Real materials are defined as much by their defects as by their ideal structure, and most of the interesting simulation questions concern them. Defects are classified by dimension. Dimension Defect Typical simulation question 0D (point) Vacancy, self-interstitial, substitutional or interstitial solute, antisite Formation energy, migration barrier, binding to other defects, concentration at equilibrium 1D (line) Edge, screw and mixed dislocations; disclinations Core structure, Peierls stress, interaction with solutes and precipitates 2D (planar) Grain boundaries, stacking faults, twin boundaries, phase boundaries, free surfaces Interface energy, segregation, sliding and migration, fracture energy 3D (volume) Voids, precipitates, inclusions Nucleation barrier, coherency strain, growth rate A point defect’s equilibrium concentration follows from the competition between the energy it costs and the configurational entropy it gains: c v ≈ exp( −E f / k B T ) (times a vibrational-entropy factor) Because the formation energy E f of a vacancy is of order 1 eV in many metals, equilibrium vacancy fractions near the melting point are about 10 −4 to 10 −3 and fall steeply on cooling. Computing E f from DFT or an interatomic potential is one of the most common first tasks in atomistic modelling."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"6. Building supercells","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#supercell","x":"A simulation cell cannot be infinite, so a supercell is built by repeating the unit cell n 1 × n 2 × n 3 times and then modifying it, for example by removing one atom to create a vacancy. Because the supercell is itself periodically repeated, the defect interacts with its own periodic images. The artefact shrinks as the cell grows, and the cost grows too, so supercell size is a convergence parameter (see the convergence section of the density functional theory page). The defect concentration implied by a single vacancy in a n × n × n fcc supercell is 1/(4 n ³): fcc supercell Atoms (before removing one) Vacancy concentration 2×2×2 32 3.1% 3×3×3 108 0.93% 4×4×4 256 0.39% 6×6×6 864 0.12% These are far above the equilibrium concentration, so the vacancies are strongly interacting in small cells: elastic strain fields overlap and the computed formation energy drifts with size. The standard remedy is to repeat the calculation in several sizes and extrapolate, or to use a cell large enough that the result changes by less than the accuracy required. For charged defects in insulators, long-range Coulomb interactions converge very slowly and need explicit correction schemes. A supercell does not have to be a simple multiple. A general transformation matrix M (integer entries) gives new lattice vectors a ′ = M a , which allows a nearly cubic cell to be built from a hexagonal or primitive fcc cell, or a cell oriented along a chosen crystallographic direction. For dislocations and grain boundaries, the cell axes are chosen along specific directions so that the periodic boundary can accommodate the defect geometry. Here is how a 4×4×4 copper cell with one vacancy looks in the Atomic Simulation Environment (ASE), a Python library: from ase.build import bulk cell = bulk(\"Cu\", \"fcc\", a=3.61, cubic=True).repeat((4, 4, 4)) # 256 atoms del cell[0] # one vacancy: 255 atoms print(len(cell), cell.get_volume())"},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"7. Periodic boundary conditions","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#pbc","x":"With periodic boundary conditions (PBC) the cell is surrounded by an infinite array of copies of itself. An atom that leaves through one face re-enters through the opposite face, so the simulation has no surfaces and a small number of atoms mimics bulk. Follow the highlighted atom in the figure: it leaves on the right and reappears on the left. The simulation cell (solid outline) and two of its periodic images (dashed). The same atoms appear in every image. Minimum image convention Each atom should interact with the nearest image of every other atom, not with a distant one. For an orthogonal box of side L , the separation component is wrapped back into the interval [− L /2, L /2]: Δx ← Δx − L · round(Δx / L) This is only correct if every interaction cutoff r c satisfies r c < L /2 (more generally, less than half the smallest perpendicular width of a triclinic cell). Otherwise an atom could interact with two images of the same neighbour. A typical metallic potential has a cutoff of 5 to 6 Å, so each box edge must exceed about 10 to 12 Å. What periodicity imposes Size limit on correlations. No fluctuation or structure can have a wavelength longer than the box. Near phase transitions, where correlation lengths diverge, results depend strongly on box size. Image interactions. Defects, charges and strain fields interact with their images, as in Section 6. Net momentum and drift. Total momentum is conserved in MD with PBC and should be set to zero; the centre of mass otherwise drifts. Diffusion bookkeeping. To compute diffusion from displacements, positions must be unwrapped (a running count of boundary crossings is kept), otherwise atoms appear to jump across the box. Compatibility of the defect with the box. A grain boundary must be compatible with the periodicity in the plane, and a single dislocation cannot be placed in a fully periodic cell because it would leave a net Burgers vector; dislocation dipoles or quadrupoles are used instead, so that opposite dislocations cancel."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"8. Surfaces and interfaces","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#surfaces","x":"To model a surface, periodicity is kept in two directions and broken in the third by adding a vacuum layer . This is called a slab model. The slab must be thick enough that the two surfaces do not interact (the middle layers should look like bulk), and the vacuum must be wide enough, often 10 Å or more, that neighbouring slabs do not interact through the vacuum. Surface energy follows from γ = ( E slab − N E bulk ) / (2A) where E bulk is the energy per atom of the bulk crystal, N the number of atoms in the slab, A the surface area and the factor 2 counts both faces. Using the same computational settings for the slab and the bulk reference is essential, otherwise numerical differences swamp the small difference being measured. For grain boundaries , two crystals with relative misorientation are joined. Geometric descriptions such as the coincidence site lattice (Σ) count how many lattice points of the two grains coincide: low-Σ boundaries (for example the Σ3 coherent twin) have high symmetry and low energy. A fully periodic cell contains two boundaries (the second appears where the two grains meet across the periodic boundary), so the excess energy is divided by twice the area. Many initial atomic arrangements (rigid-body translations, atom deletion) should be relaxed and the lowest-energy result kept, because boundary structure is a global-optimisation problem."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"9. Tools and file formats","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#tools","x":"Building and manipulating: ASE and pymatgen (Python), Atomsk (command line, good for dislocations and polycrystals), VESTA (visual). Visualisation and analysis: OVITO (defect and structure identification such as common neighbour analysis and dislocation extraction), VESTA, VMD. Common file formats: CIF (crystallographic information file, from experiment databases), POSCAR (VASP), LAMMPS data files, XYZ (plain coordinates), and the formats of Quantum ESPRESSO inputs. ASE reads and converts between most of them. Symmetry analysis: spglib (used inside ASE and pymatgen) identifies space groups and primitive cells from coordinates. Key takeaways A crystal is a lattice plus a basis. Count atoms and neighbours to sanity-check a structure: fcc has 4 atoms per cubic cell and 12 neighbours, bcc has 2 and 8. Miller indices label planes and directions; the reciprocal lattice links real-space cells to diffraction and to k-point sampling. Defects are the subject of most simulations. Supercell size is a convergence parameter, and one vacancy in an N-atom cell is a concentration of 1/N. Periodic boundaries remove walls but make a defect interact with its own images; every cutoff must be smaller than half the box. Surfaces use slabs with vacuum; a grain boundary in a fully periodic cell always comes as a pair."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#check","x":"How many atoms are in a 5×5×5 bcc supercell, and what is the concentration from one vacancy? The conventional bcc cell holds 2 atoms, so the supercell has 2 × 125 = 250 atoms. One vacancy gives 1/250 = 0.4%. Why must a box edge be larger than twice the cutoff radius? The minimum image convention picks only the nearest copy of each neighbour. If the cutoff exceeded half the box edge, an atom could be within range of two images of the same neighbour, and the force would be counted wrongly or only once. A surface-energy calculation gives a negative value. What should you check first? Whether the slab and the bulk reference were computed with identical settings (cutoff, k -point density, number of layers, smearing). Inconsistent settings are the usual cause, because the quantity being computed is a small difference of two large energies."},{"n":2,"t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#refs","x":"Kittel, C., Introduction to Solid State Physics , Wiley. Ashcroft, N. W. and Mermin, N. D., Solid State Physics , Saunders. Hammond, C., The Basics of Crystallography and Diffraction , Oxford University Press. Hirth, J. P. and Lothe, J., Theory of Dislocations , Krieger. Larsen, A. H. et al., The Atomic Simulation Environment, J. Phys.: Condens. Matter 29, 273002 (2017). Hirel, P., Atomsk: a tool for manipulating and converting atomic data files, Comput. Phys. Commun. 197, 212 (2015). Stukowski, A., Visualization and analysis of atomistic simulation data with OVITO, Modelling Simul. Mater. Sci. Eng. 18, 015012 (2010). Previous topic Introduction to computational materials science Next topic Statistical mechanics for simulation"},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html","x":"Topics / Foundations Topic 3 Statistical mechanics for simulation A simulation follows a few thousand atoms; a material has 10 23 . Statistical mechanics is the bridge: it says which averages over the small system reproduce the temperature, pressure, free energy and transport coefficients of the large one. Reading time about 12 min Builds on 1. Introduction Leads to 4. Thermodynamics and CALPHAD 6. Molecular dynamics 7. Monte Carlo and kMC Category Foundations After reading this page you should be able to: Distinguish microstates from macrostates and time averages from ensemble averages. State the main ensembles, their fixed variables and their thermodynamic potentials. Write and use the Boltzmann distribution and the canonical partition function. Explain why free-energy differences are harder to compute than energies, and name the main methods. Estimate the statistical uncertainty of a simulated average."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"1. From atoms to properties","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#bridge","x":"A microstate is a complete specification of the system at the atomic level: all positions and momenta for a classical system, or a full quantum state. A macrostate is what an experiment sees: a few variables such as temperature T , pressure p , volume V , number of atoms N and composition. Enormously many microstates correspond to one macrostate, and the macroscopic property is the average of the microscopic quantity over those microstates, weighted by how probable each is. For N classical atoms the microstate is a point in phase space of 6 N dimensions, ( r 1 , …, r N , p 1 , …, p N ). The total energy as a function of that point is the Hamiltonian, H(r, p) = Σ i |p i |² / 2m i + U(r 1 , …, r N ) Two kinds of average appear in simulation. An ensemble average ⟨ A ⟩ weights every phase-space point by its probability. A time average follows one trajectory and averages over time. Molecular dynamics produces time averages; Monte Carlo produces samples that estimate ensemble averages directly. The two agree if the system is ergodic (Section 8)."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"2. Ensembles","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#ensembles","x":"An ensemble is the set of microstates consistent with a set of fixed macroscopic conditions. Which one to use depends on what the experiment holds constant and what the algorithm can impose. Ensemble Fixed Fluctuates Potential that is minimised at equilibrium Typical use Microcanonical (NVE) N, V, E T, p Entropy S is maximised (S = k B ln Ω) Plain MD without a thermostat; testing energy conservation Canonical (NVT) N, V, T E, p Helmholtz free energy F = U − TS Properties at fixed volume; Monte Carlo Isothermal–isobaric (NPT) N, p, T E, V Gibbs free energy G = H − TS Matching laboratory conditions; thermal expansion; phase stability Grand canonical (μVT) μ, V, T E, N Grand potential Ω = F − μN Adsorption, segregation, chemical equilibrium with a reservoir In the thermodynamic limit (large N ) the ensembles give the same averages. Fluctuations of an extensive quantity scale as 1/√ N relative to its mean, so they are tiny for a macroscopic sample but visible in a box of a few hundred atoms, and they differ between ensembles. A well-posed simulation states which ensemble it samples."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"3. The Boltzmann distribution","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#boltzmann","x":"For a system in contact with a heat bath at temperature T (canonical ensemble), the probability of finding it in a microstate of energy E i is p i = exp(−E i / k B T) / Z, Z = Σ i exp(−E i / k B T) This single formula underlies Metropolis Monte Carlo, thermostatted molecular dynamics, transition-state theory and the equilibrium populations of defects and phases. Lower-energy states are more probable, but the number of states at each energy (the degeneracy) also counts: a high-energy level with many states can hold more total probability than a low-energy level with few. The balance between these two effects is the balance between energy and entropy. Populations of six equally spaced energy levels as temperature changes. The scale of “hot” and “cold” is set by the spacing ε compared with k B T. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"4. Partition function and examples","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#partition","x":"The partition function Z is the sum above, and it generates the thermodynamics. With β = 1/ k B T : F = −k B T ln Z, ⟨E⟩ = −∂(ln Z)/∂β, S = −∂F/∂T, p = −∂F/∂V If Z can be computed, everything follows. It can be computed exactly only for simple models, which are nevertheless the building blocks of larger ones. Two-level system A defect with ground state at 0 and an excited state at ε has Z = 1 + exp(−ε/ k B T ). The excited fraction is exp(−ε/ k B T )/ Z , which at low temperature gives the Arrhenius form used for equilibrium vacancy concentration. Harmonic oscillator and equipartition For one classical oscillator Z ∝ k B T /(ℏω), giving mean energy k B T : equipartition assigns ½ k B T to every quadratic term in the Hamiltonian. A solid of N atoms has 3 N oscillators and so heat capacity 3 Nk B (3 R ≈ 24.9 J mol −1 K −1 , the Dulong–Petit value). This is why classical simulations give a constant heat capacity at all temperatures, while real solids drop below it at low temperature because quantum oscillators with ℏω > k B T are frozen out. Classical MD is therefore reliable only when T is comparable to or above the Debye temperature of the material, or when quantum corrections are applied. Kinetic temperature Equipartition also defines the temperature seen in an MD run: ⟨K⟩ = ½ N f k B T, N f = 3N − 3 (for fixed total momentum; subtract further constraints) The instantaneous kinetic temperature fluctuates by a relative amount of order √(2/ N f ) in a small box, so a reading of a few percent away from the target in a 200-atom cell is normal, not an error."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"5. Free energy and entropy","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#free-energy","x":"Phase stability, defect concentrations, solubility, nucleation barriers and reaction equilibria are governed by free energies , not energies. Free energies are harder to compute because they depend on the volume of phase space accessible, not just on averages of observables. In a simulation, regions of high entropy are visited often and are easy to see, but the free energy needs the absolute probability, which is ln Z , and Z is not an average of anything that is directly sampled. Contributions to the entropy of a material include vibrational (the main finite-temperature term in most solids), configurational (arrangement of atoms or vacancies on a lattice), electronic (important in metals) and magnetic (important in iron and its alloys, where it changes the relative stability of bcc and fcc). Neglecting the right one is a common reason that a 0 K energy ranking fails at the temperature of interest. Methods for free-energy differences Harmonic and quasi-harmonic approximation. The vibrational free energy of a crystal from its phonon frequencies ω i : F vib = Σ i [½ℏω i + k B T ln(1 − exp(−ℏω i / k B T ))]. The quasi-harmonic version lets frequencies depend on volume, capturing thermal expansion. It fails when anharmonicity is strong. Thermodynamic integration. Switch the Hamiltonian from a reference H 0 to the target H 1 through a coupling parameter λ, and integrate: Δ F = ∫ 0 1 ⟨∂ H /∂λ⟩ λ dλ. With an Einstein crystal as reference this is the Frenkel–Ladd method for absolute free energies of solids; melting points follow from equating solid and liquid free energies. Free-energy perturbation. Δ F = − k B T ln⟨exp(−Δ U / k B T )⟩ 0 , accurate only when the two states overlap strongly. Umbrella sampling and metadynamics. To obtain a free-energy profile along a coordinate (for example the distance an atom moves, or a nucleation order parameter), bias the simulation so that it samples barriers and then remove the bias. Both rely on a good choice of coordinate. Coexistence methods. Simulate two phases in one box and watch the interface move or stay still; the temperature at which it is stationary is the transition temperature."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"6. Properties from fluctuations","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#fluctuations","x":"Response functions are determined by fluctuations of the corresponding quantities at equilibrium. For example in the canonical ensemble, C V = ( ⟨E²⟩ − ⟨E⟩² ) / (k B T²) and in the isothermal–isobaric ensemble the isothermal compressibility follows from the variance of the volume, κ T = (⟨ V ²⟩ − ⟨ V ⟩²)/( k B T ⟨ V ⟩). Thermal expansion comes from the cross-fluctuation of enthalpy and volume, and elastic constants from stress fluctuations. These formulae let one run produce several properties, but they converge slowly because they are variances; direct perturbation (apply a small strain and measure the response) is often more efficient."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"7. Correlation functions and transport","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#correlation","x":"Structure. The radial distribution function g ( r ) is the probability, relative to a uniform gas, of finding an atom a distance r from another. Crystals give sharp peaks, liquids give damped oscillations that decay to 1, and amorphous solids lie between. Its Fourier transform is the structure factor S ( q ), directly comparable with diffraction. The coordination number is the integral of 4π r ²ρ g ( r ) up to the first minimum. Dynamics. Time-correlation functions measure how quickly the system forgets a state. Transport coefficients follow from them. The diffusion coefficient has two equivalent forms: Einstein: D = lim t→∞ ⟨|r(t) − r(0)|²⟩ / (6t) Green–Kubo: D = (1/3) ∫ 0 ∞ ⟨v(0)·v(t)⟩ dt The mean-squared displacement (MSD) must be computed from unwrapped coordinates, and the slope fitted only in the linear (diffusive) regime, not the early ballistic regime where MSD ∝ t ². Viscosity and thermal conductivity have analogous Green–Kubo expressions (from stress and heat-current correlations), and these converge slowly."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"8. Ergodicity and sampling","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#ergodicity","x":"A system is ergodic if, given enough time, a trajectory visits all accessible regions of phase space in proportion to their probability. Then the time average equals the ensemble average. Real simulations are finite, and several situations break this in practice: Rare events. A system with an energy barrier much larger than k B T can sit in one basin for longer than the run, so the average describes only that basin. With a typical attempt frequency of 10 13 s −1 , an atomic hop over a 1 eV barrier at 300 K occurs about once every 10 4 seconds (hours), compared with MD runs of nanoseconds, a gap of some thirteen orders of magnitude. Metastability and hysteresis. Heating and cooling runs through a phase transition give different answers because nucleation is slow. Glassy dynamics. Structural relaxation is much slower than the simulated time. Remedies are: run longer; start from several independent configurations and compare; use methods that sample differently (Monte Carlo with swap moves, replica exchange, which exchanges configurations between temperatures; metadynamics; accelerated dynamics); or use kinetic methods that model rare events explicitly (see the Monte Carlo page)."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"9. Statistical error","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#errors","x":"A simulated average is an estimate with an uncertainty, and a number without an error bar is incomplete. Successive configurations in an MD or MC run are correlated , so the number of independent samples is much smaller than the number of recorded frames. For M frames with correlation time τ (in units of the sampling interval), the standard error of the mean is approximately σ mean ≈ σ √( 2τ / M ) where σ is the standard deviation of the quantity. Practical ways to get it: Block averaging. Divide the series into blocks longer than the correlation time, average each block and compute the error from the block means. Increase the block length until the estimated error stops growing. Independent replicas. Repeat with different random seeds or initial velocities; the spread of results is the error. Discard the equilibration period. The start of a run depends on the initial configuration; plot the property against time and start averaging only after it has stopped drifting. Systematic errors (a poor potential, finite box, neglected quantum effects) are not reduced by longer runs and are usually larger than the statistical error. Report both kinds. Key takeaways The Boltzmann distribution, p proportional to exp(-E/kT), underlies Metropolis Monte Carlo, thermostats and equilibrium defect concentrations. Pick the ensemble that matches what is held fixed: NVE, NVT, NPT or the grand canonical. Free energies, not energies, decide stability, and they need dedicated methods: thermodynamic integration, perturbation, umbrella sampling, the quasi-harmonic approximation. Classical simulation assumes equipartition, so it overestimates heat capacity when quantum modes are frozen out at low temperature. Quote averages with error bars. The correlation time sets the number of independent samples, and ergodicity is never guaranteed."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#check","x":"At what temperature is the excited state of a two-level defect with ε = 1 eV populated at 1%? Solve exp(−ε/ k B T )/(1 + exp(−ε/ k B T )) = 0.01, which gives ε/ k B T ≈ 4.6, so T ≈ 11 600 K / 4.6 ≈ 2 500 K (using 1 eV/ k B ≈ 11 600 K). Even at high temperature the excited state stays rare for such a large gap. Why does classical MD overestimate the heat capacity of a solid at low temperature? Classical equipartition gives each oscillator energy k B T at every temperature, so C V = 3 Nk B . In a real solid, modes with ℏω larger than k B T are not excited, so C V falls below this value as the temperature drops. A run of 10 6 frames has a correlation time of 10 3 frames. About how many independent samples is that? Roughly M /(2τ) = 10 6 /(2×10 3 ) = 500 independent samples, so the standard error is about σ/√500, not σ/√10 6 ."},{"n":3,"t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#refs","x":"Chandler, D., Introduction to Modern Statistical Mechanics , Oxford University Press. McQuarrie, D. A., Statistical Mechanics , University Science Books. Tuckerman, M. E., Statistical Mechanics: Theory and Molecular Simulation , Oxford University Press. Frenkel, D. and Smit, B., Understanding Molecular Simulation , Academic Press (free energies, ensembles, error analysis). Frenkel, D. and Ladd, A. J. C., New Monte Carlo method to compute the free energy of arbitrary solids, J. Chem. Phys. 81, 3188 (1984). Flyvbjerg, H. and Petersen, H. G., Error estimates on averages of correlated data, J. Chem. Phys. 91, 461 (1989). Laio, A. and Parrinello, M., Escaping free-energy minima, Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA 99, 12562 (2002). Previous topic Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries Next topic Thermodynamics and CALPHAD"},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html","x":"Topics / Thermodynamics Topic 4 Thermodynamics and CALPHAD Before asking how fast anything happens, ask what is stable. The CALPHAD method turns measured and computed thermodynamic data into models of the Gibbs energy of every phase, and from those models any equilibrium can be calculated for any composition. Reading time about 12 min Builds on 3. Statistical mechanics Leads to 8. Diffusion and kinetics 9. Phase-field Category Thermodynamics After reading this page you should be able to: State the condition for equilibrium in terms of Gibbs energy and chemical potentials. Write the Gibbs energy of ideal and regular solutions and predict a miscibility gap. Construct the common tangent and apply the lever rule. Describe how CALPHAD models phases, and list what the method can and cannot calculate."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"1. Equilibrium and Gibbs energy","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#basics","x":"At constant temperature T and pressure p , a closed system is at equilibrium when its Gibbs energy is a minimum: G = H − TS = U + pV − TS The enthalpy H favours strong bonding and the entropy term − TS favours disorder, and rising temperature shifts the balance toward entropy. For a multicomponent system the contribution of each component is its chemical potential μ i = (∂ G /∂ n i ) T , p , n j≠i . Equilibrium between phases then requires that each component has the same chemical potential in every phase in which it is present: μ i α = μ i β = … for every component i The Gibbs phase rule counts how many variables may be changed independently: F = C − P + 2 with pressure free, or F = C − P + 1 at fixed pressure, where C is the number of components and P the number of phases. In a binary alloy at constant pressure, three phases can coexist only at one temperature ( F = 0), which is why eutectic, eutectoid and peritectic reactions occur at fixed temperatures."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"2. Mixing and solution models","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#mixing","x":"The Gibbs energy of one mole of a binary solution of A and B is built from three parts: G = x A G A 0 + x B G B 0 + ΔG mix , ΔG mix = RT (x A ln x A + x B ln x B ) + G ex The first two terms are the pure components in the same structure; the logarithmic term is the ideal entropy of mixing (− R Σ x ln x per mole), always favouring mixing and with infinite slope at the pure ends, which is why no phase is perfectly pure; and G ex is the excess Gibbs energy that collects all non-ideal interactions. The simplest form is the regular solution G ex = Ω x A x B , where Ω > 0 means unlike neighbours are disfavoured (tendency to separate) and Ω < 0 means they are favoured (tendency to order or form compounds). The activity a i = exp[(μ i − μ i 0 )/ RT ] = γ i x i measures the effective concentration. The activity coefficient γ i equals 1 for an ideal solution; Raoult’s law holds for the solvent and Henry’s law ( a i ∝ x i ) for a dilute solute, with γ constant but not 1."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"3. Common tangent and stability","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#tangent","x":"For a regular solution with Ω > 0 the shape of the Gibbs energy curve changes with temperature: at high temperature the entropy term wins and the curve has a single minimum, while at low temperature the curve has two minima separated by a hump. Watch it happen in the animation: the cooling solution develops a miscibility gap. Regular-solution Gibbs energy of mixing as temperature falls (hot to cool, gauge on the right). Below the critical temperature, the dashed line is the common tangent and the composition range between its touching points is the miscibility gap. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. For a regular solution the critical (consolute) temperature, above which the gap closes, is T c = Ω / (2R) Below T c an alloy of intermediate composition lowers its Gibbs energy by splitting into two phases. The compositions of the two phases are found by the common tangent construction : draw the line that touches the curve at two points. The tangent condition is exactly equal chemical potentials in the two phases (the intercepts of the tangent with the pure-component axes are μ A and μ B ). For a binary mixture of overall composition x 0 splitting into phases of compositions x α and x β , the lever rule gives the fractions: f β = (x 0 − x α ) / (x β − x α ), f α = 1 − f β Binodal and spinodal Within the gap there are two kinds of instability. Between the common-tangent points (the binodal ) the two-phase state is stable, but a homogeneous solution may still be locally stable and decompose only after nucleation of a second phase. Inside the spinodal , where ∂² G /∂ x ² < 0, the solution is unstable to any small composition fluctuation and decomposes spontaneously. The distinction matters for kinetics and for simulation: the spinodal mechanism is the one modelled in the phase-field topic."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"4. Reading phase diagrams","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#diagrams","x":"A binary phase diagram is a map of the stable phases against temperature and composition, produced by repeating the common-tangent construction at each temperature. Boundaries are loci of tangent points. A few conventions let you read any diagram: In a single-phase region, the phase composition equals the overall composition. In a two-phase region, draw a horizontal tie line at the temperature; its ends on the phase boundaries give the phase compositions, and the lever rule gives the amounts. Three-phase equilibria appear as horizontal lines (invariant reactions): eutectic L → α + β, eutectoid γ → α + β, peritectic L + α → β. Ternary systems are shown as isothermal sections or vertical sections (isopleths); tie lines in a ternary two-phase region do not generally lie along a fixed direction, which is why calculation is needed. The iron–carbon system illustrates the points. The eutectoid at about 727 °C and 0.76 wt% C converts austenite (fcc) into ferrite (bcc, almost no carbon) and cementite (Fe 3 C, 6.67 wt% C). A 0.40 wt% C steel just below 727 °C contains, by the lever rule, a cementite fraction of (0.40 − 0.02)/(6.67 − 0.02) ≈ 5.7%. Note that Fe–Fe 3 C is a metastable diagram: graphite is the true equilibrium phase, but cementite forms because its nucleation is much easier."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"5. The CALPHAD method","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#calphad","x":"Phase diagrams were originally drawn from measurements alone. CALPHAD (CALculation of PHAse Diagrams, developed from the 1970s, building on Kaufman and Bernstein) replaces drawing with modelling: every phase gets an analytical expression for its Gibbs energy as a function of T , p and composition, with adjustable parameters. The workflow is: Collect data for the system: phase boundaries, invariant temperatures, heats of formation and mixing, activities, heat capacities, plus first-principles (DFT) formation energies for compounds that are hard to measure. Choose models for each phase that match its crystal structure and chemistry (Section 6). Optimise parameters so that the combined calculation reproduces all data within experimental uncertainty. Binary systems are assessed first, then ternaries, and the models are extrapolated to higher-order systems. Store the parameters in a thermodynamic database. The common exchange format is the TDB text file, readable by several programs. Calculate equilibria by minimising total Gibbs energy under the imposed conditions. The strength of the approach is that the same expression describes stable and metastable states and extends into regions that were never measured; its weakness is that the result is only as good as the assessment (Section 9)."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"6. Models inside a database","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#models","x":"Pure elements Each element in each structure (even structures it never adopts, such as bcc for a metal that is hcp) has a Gibbs energy function written as a polynomial in T , relative to a reference state. The SGTE unary database, a community standard, supplies these lattice stabilities in the form G(T) = a + bT + cT ln T + dT² + eT −1 + fT³ + … Substitutional solutions Solution phases with atoms mixing on one set of sites are written as the sum of the three terms in Section 2, with the excess term expanded in a Redlich–Kister polynomial : G ex = x A x B Σ k k L AB (x A − x B ) k , k L = a k + b k T The term with k = 0 is the regular-solution parameter; k = 1 introduces asymmetry. The parameters a k and b k are the quantities fitted during assessment. Higher-order systems add ternary interaction terms, and binary data are extrapolated to ternaries with symmetric or asymmetric schemes (Muggianu, Kohler, Toop). Sublattice models Interstitial solutions and compounds with more than one type of site are written with the compound energy formalism (Hillert and Staffansson). Each sublattice has its own set of species, including vacancies (Va). For austenite or ferrite in the Fe–C system, carbon occupies interstitial sites that are mostly empty, giving the model (Fe) a (C, Va) c , with a = c = 1 for fcc and a = 1, c = 3 for bcc. Intermetallics such as Fe 3 C or sigma phase use sublattices with composition-dependent occupancy, and a stoichiometric compound is the limiting case with fixed site fractions. Magnetism and ordering Ferromagnetic transitions change the Gibbs energy by an amount large enough to move phase boundaries (it is why iron is bcc at low temperature and fcc between about 912 and 1394 °C). They are included with a separate magnetic term based on the Curie temperature and the Bohr magneton number (Inden–Hillert–Jarl model). Chemical ordering, for example B2 from bcc, is handled by using the same Gibbs energy for the ordered and disordered phases with an additional sublattice description for the ordered state."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"7. What can be calculated","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#calc","x":"Calculation What it gives Typical use Equilibrium at fixed T, p, composition Stable phases, their amounts and compositions Heat treatment windows, solubility limits, second-phase amounts Property diagram (phase fraction versus T) Transformation temperatures, fraction curves Solvus, solidus and liquidus; A e1 and A e3 of steels Phase diagram and isopleth Phase boundaries in a plane of composition and T Alloy design; checking the effect of a new addition Scheil–Gulliver solidification Solidification path assuming no diffusion in the solid and complete mixing in the liquid Segregation, last-liquid composition, hot-cracking tendency Constrained equilibrium (for example paraequilibrium) Equilibrium with some elements held fixed, such as interstitial carbon moving while substitutional atoms do not Fast transformations, bainite and martensite thermodynamics Driving force Gibbs energy change for forming a new phase from the current state Nucleation models; precipitation and phase-field inputs Activities and chemical potentials Effective concentrations Slag–metal reactions, oxidation, diffusion couples"},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"8. A short calculation","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#python","x":"The open-source Python package pycalphad calculates equilibria from a TDB database. The sketch below finds the equilibrium phases of a 2 at.% carbon iron alloy over a temperature range, assuming you have a suitable Fe–C database file: from pycalphad import Database, equilibrium, variables as v dbf = Database(\"Fe-C.tdb\") # your thermodynamic database comps = [\"FE\", \"C\", \"VA\"] phases = [\"LIQUID\", \"BCC_A2\", \"FCC_A1\", \"CEMENTITE\"] eq = equilibrium( dbf, comps, phases, {v.X(\"C\"): 0.02, v.T: (600, 1800, 10), v.P: 101325, v.N: 1}, ) # eq.Phase, eq.NP (phase fractions) and eq.X (compositions) vs temperature Phase names depend on the database. A database is licensed data: some are free and published with the assessment, others are commercial. Always check which composition and temperature ranges a database was assessed for before trusting an answer outside them."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"9. Limits and coupling","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#limits","x":"Equilibrium only. CALPHAD has no time, geometry or interfaces. It says where a system is heading, not how fast or in what shape. Database quality. Parameters are fitted to the data that existed; predictions outside the assessed range, or for a phase with sparse data, rest on extrapolation. A ternary or higher-order prediction made from binary data alone can be wrong when real ternary interactions are strong. Missing phases. If a phase is absent from the database (or from your list of candidate phases), it cannot be predicted. Metastable phases may be included deliberately. Local equilibrium assumption. Used in diffusion calculations, it holds when interfaces are fast compared with diffusion. Coupling extends what CALPHAD can do. Diffusion mobility databases assessed in the same way as thermodynamics (used in DICTRA and similar programs) enable one-dimensional simulation of diffusion-controlled transformations and homogenisation. Phase-field models take their free-energy densities, driving forces and chemical potentials from CALPHAD. DFT supplies formation energies of compounds and end-member energies. The thermodynamic input of larger models is therefore often a CALPHAD calculation. Key takeaways Equilibrium means minimum Gibbs energy, equivalently equal chemical potentials. The common tangent gives phase compositions and the lever rule gives amounts. For a regular solution the miscibility gap closes at Tc = Omega/2R; the spinodal lies inside the binodal. CALPHAD fits a Gibbs energy model to every phase (Redlich-Kister terms, sublattices, magnetism) and stores the parameters in a database. It delivers equilibria, solidification paths and driving forces. It does not deliver kinetics or geometry. Database coverage limits accuracy, and multicomponent extrapolation from binary data needs checking."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#check","x":"A regular solution has Ω = 20 kJ/mol. At what temperature does the miscibility gap close? T c = Ω/(2 R ) = 20 000/(2 × 8.314) ≈ 1 200 K. An alloy with 30 at.% B splits into phases of 10 at.% and 70 at.% B. What fraction is the B-rich phase? Lever rule: (30 − 10)/(70 − 10) = 20/60 ≈ 33%. Why does the ideal entropy of mixing make perfectly pure phases impossible? The slope of RT ( x ln x + (1 − x ) ln(1 − x )) is infinite at x = 0 and x = 1, so adding even a tiny amount of a second component always lowers the Gibbs energy. Every real phase therefore dissolves some of every component, however small."},{"n":4,"t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#refs","x":"Lukas, H. L., Fries, S. G. and Sundman, B., Computational Thermodynamics: The Calphad Method , Cambridge University Press (2007). Saunders, N. and Miodownik, A. P., CALPHAD: Calculation of Phase Diagrams , Pergamon (1998). Hillert, M., Phase Equilibria, Phase Diagrams and Phase Transformations , Cambridge University Press. Gaskell, D. R. and Laughlin, D. E., Introduction to the Thermodynamics of Materials , CRC Press. Kaufman, L. and Bernstein, H., Computer Calculation of Phase Diagrams , Academic Press (1970). Dinsdale, A. T., SGTE data for pure elements, Calphad 15, 317 (1991). Hillert, M. and Staffansson, L.-I., The regular solution model for stoichiometric phases and ionic melts, Acta Chem. Scand. 24, 3618 (1970). Otis, R. and Liu, Z.-K., pycalphad: CALPHAD-based computational thermodynamics in Python, J. Open Res. Softw. 5, 1 (2017). Bocklund, B. et al., ESPEI for efficient thermodynamic database development, modification and uncertainty quantification, MRS Commun. 9, 618 (2019). Previous topic Statistical mechanics for simulation Next topic Density functional theory in practice"},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"Density functional theory in practice","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html","x":"Topics / Electrons Topic 5 Density functional theory in practice Density functional theory (DFT) computes the energy and electronic structure of a solid from its atomic positions alone. It is the workhorse behind most first-principles materials data: formation energies, elastic constants, defect energies, band structures and the training sets for machine-learned potentials. Reading time about 13 min Builds on 2. Crystals and cells Leads to 11. Machine learning Category Electrons After reading this page you should be able to: Explain the Kohn–Sham idea and the role of the exchange–correlation functional. Choose among common functionals and know the typical errors of each. List the numerical parameters that must be converged and how to test them. Describe how to obtain lattice parameters, elastic constants, defect energies, barriers and phonons from DFT."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"1. The electronic structure problem","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#problem","x":"The nuclei of a solid are about 2000 times heavier than electrons, so electrons respond almost instantly to nuclear motion. In the Born–Oppenheimer approximation the nuclei are fixed and the electrons are solved for each arrangement, giving an energy surface E ( R 1 , …, R N ) on which the nuclei move; its negative gradients are the forces. This surface is what every classical interatomic potential tries to approximate. The electrons obey the many-body Schrödinger equation, whose wavefunction depends on 3 coordinates per electron. Exact solutions are impossible beyond a few electrons. DFT replaces the wavefunction with the electron density n ( r ), a function of only three coordinates no matter how many electrons there are."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"2. The Kohn–Sham scheme","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#ks","x":"The Hohenberg–Kohn theorems (1964) prove that the ground-state energy is a functional of the density and is minimised by the true density. They do not say what the functional is. Kohn and Sham (1965) made it usable by mapping the interacting system onto fictitious non-interacting electrons that have the same density, and splitting the energy as E[n] = T s [n] + ∫ v ext (r) n(r) dr + E H [n] + E xc [n] The terms are, in order: the kinetic energy of the non-interacting electrons, the energy in the potential of the nuclei, the classical electrostatic (Hartree) energy of the density with itself, and the exchange–correlation energy E xc , which contains the quantum exchange of identical electrons, their correlated motion, and the correction to the kinetic energy. Minimising E [ n ] gives one-electron equations: [ −½∇² + v eff (r) ] ψ i (r) = ε i ψ i (r), v eff = v ext + v H [n] + v xc [n], n(r) = Σ occ |ψ i (r)|² (in atomic units). The orbitals ψ i and eigenvalues ε i are mathematical devices to build the density; only the total energy and the density have exact meaning. Reading band structures from the Kohn–Sham eigenvalues is a useful but approximate practice."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"3. Exchange–correlation functionals","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#xc","x":"Everything not computed exactly is in E xc , which must be approximated. The choice of approximation is the main modelling decision in a DFT calculation, climbing a ladder of increasing ingredients and cost. Level Depends on Examples Typical behaviour Local density (LDA) Density at a point Ceperley–Alder / PZ Lattice parameters too small by 1–3%; bonds too strong; wrong ground state for iron Generalised gradient (GGA) Density and its gradient PBE, PBEsol, PW91 PBE lattice parameters 1–2% too large; PBEsol closer to experiment for solids; correct bcc ferromagnetic Fe; band gaps too small Meta-GGA Adds kinetic-energy density SCAN, r 2 SCAN, TPSS Better energetics and some correlated systems at modest extra cost Hybrid Mixes in a fraction of exact exchange PBE0, HSE06 Much better band gaps and localised-state description; 10 to 100 times the cost of GGA in plane-wave codes Beyond Many-body perturbation GW, random-phase approximation Quasiparticle band gaps, van der Waals; very expensive Common corrections Spin polarisation. Magnetic materials such as Fe, Co, Ni and many oxides need separate spin-up and spin-down densities. The starting magnetic moments and the magnetic configuration (ferromagnetic, antiferromagnetic) must be chosen deliberately, and different starting guesses can converge to different local solutions. DFT+U. An on-site Hubbard term corrects over-delocalisation of localised d and f electrons (transition-metal oxides, rare earths). U is a parameter and results can depend on it. Dispersion. Standard functionals miss van der Waals attraction. Empirical corrections (the “D3” scheme) or non-local functionals (vdW-DF) are used for layered materials, molecular crystals and adsorption. Practical rules: PBE is the default for metals and alloys. For energy differences between similar structures the errors often cancel, so DFT is far more reliable for relative energies than for absolute ones. Formation energies from GGA typically carry errors of order 0.1 eV per atom, which is large compared with the energy differences that separate competing phases, so conclusions about borderline stability need care. Band gaps from semi-local functionals are consistently underestimated."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"4. Basis sets and pseudopotentials","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#basis","x":"The Kohn–Sham orbitals must be expanded in a finite basis. In a crystal the potential is periodic, and Bloch’s theorem says each orbital has the form ψ n k ( r ) = e i k · r u n k ( r ), with u periodic and k a point in the Brillouin zone. Two families of basis dominate: Plane waves (VASP, Quantum ESPRESSO, ABINIT, CASTEP) expand u in Fourier components exp( i G · r ) of the reciprocal lattice. The basis is controlled by one number, the cutoff energy : all plane waves with ½| k + G |² ≤ E cut are included. It is systematic: raising E cut always improves the basis. Localised orbitals (Gaussians in CP2K and Gaussian; numerical atomic orbitals in SIESTA and FHI-aims) are efficient for large and molecular systems and offer linear-scaling options, but need care over basis-set completeness. Core electrons are chemically inert but have rapidly oscillating wavefunctions that would need an enormous plane-wave basis. Pseudopotentials (and the closely related projector-augmented-wave, PAW , method) replace the nucleus and core electrons by an effective potential for the valence electrons, smooth inside a core radius. Choices include norm-conserving, ultrasoft and PAW datasets. Semi-core states (for example 3 p in transition metals) should be included in the valence set when they influence the property. All-electron methods (such as FLAPW) avoid pseudopotentials at higher cost and are used as a reference."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"5. Sampling reciprocal space","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#kpoints","x":"Many quantities (the density, the total energy) are integrals over the Brillouin zone, evaluated as a sum over a finite set of k -points. The standard choice is a uniform Monkhorst–Pack grid, reduced by symmetry. Insulators converge with a few points because their occupation is smooth. Metals have a sharp Fermi surface, so they need dense grids and a smearing function that smooths the occupation numbers: Gaussian, Methfessel–Paxton, Marzari–Vanderbilt (cold smearing) or Fermi–Dirac. Smearing introduces a small error that depends on the width; the tetrahedron method with Blöchl corrections is used for accurate densities of states. A useful rule: k -point density should scale inversely with cell size. A 4-atom cell may need a 12×12×12 grid where a 256-atom supercell of the same crystal converges with 2×2×2 or 3×3×3."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"6. The self-consistency cycle","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#scf","x":"The Kohn–Sham equations are non-linear: the potential depends on the density, which depends on the orbitals, which depend on the potential. They are solved by iteration. Guess a density, build the potential, solve for orbitals, form a new density, and repeat until input and output densities agree. The self-consistent field cycle. The dot follows one pass around the loop; each pass costs a diagonalisation of the Hamiltonian. Simply replacing the old density with the new one oscillates or diverges, so a mixing scheme (Pulay/DIIS or Broyden) combines the history of densities. Convergence is declared when the change in energy or density drops below a tolerance (for example 10 −6 eV in VASP for routine work, tighter for phonons). Slow or failed convergence usually has a physical cause: a metallic system with too little smearing, an incorrect magnetic starting guess, a nearly degenerate state, or a bad structure. Once the electronic problem is converged, forces and stress follow from the Hellmann–Feynman theorem, and the structure can be relaxed by moving atoms (and the cell) downhill in energy."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"7. Convergence","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#convergence","x":"Every numerical parameter must be tested, because a result that has not converged is a result of the parameters, not of the material. The method is the same each time: vary one parameter while holding the others fixed and watch the quantity of interest (not just the total energy). Parameter What it controls Typical test Comment Plane-wave cutoff Basis completeness Raise until energy differences change by under about 1 meV per atom Stress converges more slowly than energy; relaxing a cell needs a higher cutoff or a fixed-basis re-run k -point grid Brillouin-zone integration Increase grid density until the property changes by under tolerance Metals need more than insulators Smearing width Fermi-surface treatment Reduce width while increasing k density Report the scheme and width used Supercell size Defect–image interaction Repeat in larger cells; extrapolate See supercells and periodic boundaries Vacuum thickness, slab thickness Surface and interface models Increase until the surface energy stops changing Both matter Force and SCF tolerances Relaxation accuracy Tighten until structure and energy stop changing Typical force tolerance 0.01 eV/Å; phonons need much smaller When comparing energies of different structures, use the same cutoff, pseudopotentials and equivalent k -point density in all of them, so that errors cancel. Test with differences, not absolute energies."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"8. What to compute","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#calc","x":"Structure and equation of state Compute the total energy at several volumes (relaxing atoms at each), fit an equation of state, and read off the equilibrium volume V 0 , the bulk modulus B 0 = − V (∂ p /∂ V ) and its pressure derivative. The Birch–Murnaghan form is the standard fit: E(V) = E 0 + (9V 0 B 0 /16) { [(V 0 /V) 2/3 − 1] 3 B′ 0 + [(V 0 /V) 2/3 − 1] 2 [6 − 4(V 0 /V) 2/3 ] } Formation energies and phase stability The formation energy per atom of a compound A x B y relative to the elements in their ground-state structures is E f = [ E(A x B y ) − x E(A) − y E(B) ] / (x + y) A negative value means the compound is stable against decomposition into the elements; stability against all competing phases requires comparing with the lowest-energy combination, the convex hull . DFT formation energies are the input for many CALPHAD assessments. Elastic constants Apply small strains ε, relax atoms, and fit the stress σ = C ε. For a cubic crystal there are three independent constants C 11 , C 12 , C 44 , the bulk modulus is B = ( C 11 + 2 C 12 )/3, mechanical stability requires C 11 − C 12 > 0, C 11 + 2 C 12 > 0 and C 44 > 0, and the Zener anisotropy ratio is A = 2 C 44 /( C 11 − C 12 ). Polycrystalline moduli follow by averaging (Voigt, Reuss, Hill). Defects, interfaces and barriers The vacancy formation energy in a supercell of N atoms is E f vac = E ( N −1) − [( N −1)/ N ] E ( N ), and solute, interstitial and surface energies follow the same logic of subtracting reference energies. Energy barriers for atomic hops come from the nudged elastic band method, which relaxes a chain of images between the initial and final states while keeping them evenly spaced; the climbing-image variant (Henkelman et al.) converges the highest image onto the saddle point. For vacancy diffusion the activation energy is Q = E f + E m (formation plus migration), which compares directly with experiment. Vibrations and temperature Phonon frequencies are obtained by displacing atoms in a supercell and recording forces (finite displacements, programs such as Phonopy) or by perturbation theory. The phonon spectrum gives the vibrational free energy in the harmonic or quasi-harmonic approximation (see statistical mechanics ), thermal expansion, heat capacity and the stability of a structure: an imaginary frequency means the structure is dynamically unstable. Ab initio molecular dynamics pro"},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"9. Cost and accuracy","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#cost","x":"With conventional diagonalisation, DFT cost grows roughly as the cube of the number of atoms. Practical limits are hundreds of atoms for routine work and a few thousand with large computing resources; hybrid functionals and large k -grids make the calculation heavier by an order of magnitude or more. Linear-scaling and GPU-accelerated implementations extend the range. An important finding for reproducibility: a large community comparison of about 40 code and pseudopotential combinations on 71 elemental crystals found that modern implementations agree closely when the same functional is used, so the largest source of disagreement in a published DFT result is usually the functional and the setup, not the code (Lejaeghere et al., 2016). Known weaknesses to keep in mind: semi-local functionals under-bind and over-delocalise in strongly correlated systems; energy barriers can be underestimated; and DFT at 0 K ignores finite-temperature entropy unless added separately."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"10. Inputs in common codes","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#inputs","x":"The parameters above appear under different names in each program. The table maps a few widely used ones. Concept VASP Quantum ESPRESSO Cutoff energy ENCUT (eV) ecutwfc (Ry), ecutrho k -point grid KPOINTS file K_POINTS automatic Smearing ISMEAR , SIGMA occupations , smearing , degauss SCF tolerance EDIFF (eV) conv_thr (Ry) Spin polarisation ISPIN = 2 , MAGMOM nspin = 2 , starting_magnetization Relaxation IBRION , ISIF , NSW , EDIFFG calculation = 'relax' or 'vc-relax' Units differ (eV against Rydberg), as do conventions for the functional and pseudopotential: record these exactly, since they are part of the result. Key takeaways DFT solves for the electron density through the Kohn-Sham equations; only the exchange-correlation energy is approximated. The functional (LDA, GGA, meta-GGA, hybrid, plus U, van der Waals) sets the typical errors. Relative energies are more reliable than absolute ones. Converge the cutoff, k-points, smearing and cell size before trusting any number. Standard outputs are lattice constants, elastic constants, formation and defect energies, barriers by nudged elastic band, and phonons. Cost grows roughly as N cubed, which limits cells to hundreds or a few thousand atoms, and results are for 0 K unless extended."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"11. Check your understanding","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#check","x":"Your computed vacancy formation energy changes from 1.1 eV to 1.5 eV when you double the supercell. What does that suggest? The small cell was not converged: the vacancy was interacting with its periodic images. Keep enlarging until the value stabilises, or check convergence against both cell size and k -points, since both are changing in practice. Why must two structures being compared be computed with the same cutoff and equivalent k -point density? Basis and sampling errors are systematic and partly cancel only when they are the same in both calculations. Using different settings leaves a difference in numerical error that can exceed the physical energy difference. A phonon calculation returns an imaginary frequency. What does it mean? The structure is at a saddle point rather than a minimum of the energy surface: it is dynamically unstable and would distort spontaneously. It can also indicate an unconverged calculation, so test the convergence before drawing a physical conclusion."},{"n":5,"t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"12. References and further reading","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#refs","x":"Sholl, D. S. and Steckel, J. A., Density Functional Theory: A Practical Introduction , Wiley (2009). Martin, R. M., Electronic Structure: Basic Theory and Practical Methods , Cambridge University Press (2004). Hohenberg, P. and Kohn, W., Phys. Rev. 136, B864 (1964); Kohn, W. and Sham, L. J., Phys. Rev. 140, A1133 (1965). Perdew, J. P., Burke, K. and Ernzerhof, M., Phys. Rev. Lett. 77, 3865 (1996). Sun, J., Ruzsinszky, A. and Perdew, J. P., Strongly constrained and appropriately normed semilocal density functional, Phys. Rev. Lett. 115, 036402 (2015). Heyd, J., Scuseria, G. E. and Ernzerhof, M., Hybrid functionals based on a screened Coulomb potential, J. Chem. Phys. 118, 8207 (2003). Blöchl, P. E., Projector augmented-wave method, Phys. Rev. B 50, 17953 (1994). Monkhorst, H. J. and Pack, J. D., Special points for Brillouin-zone integrations, Phys. Rev. B 13, 5188 (1976). Kresse, G. and Furthmüller, J., Efficient iterative schemes for ab initio total-energy calculations using a plane-wave basis set, Phys. Rev. B 54, 11169 (1996). Giannozzi, P. et al., QUANTUM ESPRESSO, J. Phys.: Condens. Matter 21, 395502 (2009). Henkelman, G., Uberuaga, B. P. and Jónsson, H., A climbing image nudged elastic band method, J. Chem. Phys. 113, 9901 (2000). Togo, A. and Tanaka, I., First principles phonon calculations in materials science, Scr. Mater. 108, 1 (2015). Lejaeghere, K. et al., Reproducibility in density functional theory calculations of solids, Science 351, aad3000 (2016). Previous topic Thermodynamics and CALPHAD Next topic Molecular dynamics"},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"Molecular dynamics","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html","x":"Topics / Atoms Topic 6 Molecular dynamics Molecular dynamics (MD) is the closest thing to a movie of matter: place atoms in a box, compute the force on each from an interatomic potential, move them a tiny step, and repeat a few million times. Averages over the trajectory give thermodynamic and transport properties, and the pictures show mechanisms. Reading time about 11 min Builds on 2. Crystals and cells 3. Statistical mechanics Leads to 11. Machine learning Category Atoms After reading this page you should be able to: Write the velocity-Verlet algorithm and choose a sensible time step. Compare the main classes of interatomic potential and say how to validate one. Describe thermostats and barostats and which ensembles they produce. Plan a simulation from construction to analysis, and judge what MD can and cannot reach."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"1. The idea","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#idea","x":"Classical MD treats atoms as point masses that obey Newton’s second law under forces derived from a potential energy function U of all the positions: m i d²r i /dt² = F i = −∇ i U(r 1 , …, r N ) There are three ingredients: a potential that supplies the forces, an integrator that advances the positions and velocities, and the conditions (cell, boundaries, temperature, pressure). Starting from initial positions and random velocities, the trajectory samples the ensemble defined by those conditions (see statistical mechanics ). The following simulation shows a two-dimensional crystal of Lennard-Jones atoms being heated: atoms start vibrating about lattice sites, then the lattice loses order and atoms begin to wander. A real molecular dynamics run: 144 atoms interacting through the Lennard-Jones potential in two dimensions, periodic boundaries, temperature ramped from low to high (reduced units). Colour shows how far each atom has moved: blue atoms stay near their sites, red atoms have travelled."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"2. Integrating the equations","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#integration","x":"A good integrator should be cheap (one force evaluation per step, because forces dominate the cost), time-reversible and symplectic , so that the energy does not drift over long runs. The standard choice is the velocity-Verlet algorithm. With time step Δ t : v(t + Δt/2) = v(t) + F(t) Δt / 2m r(t + Δt) = r(t) + v(t + Δt/2) Δt compute F(t + Δt) from the new positions v(t + Δt) = v(t + Δt/2) + F(t + Δt) Δt / 2m Its error per step is of order Δ t 3 , but its structure preserves a nearby “shadow” energy, so the total energy fluctuates slightly without drifting if Δ t is small enough. Trajectories themselves are chaotic: two runs that start a hair apart diverge exponentially, so individual trajectories are not reproducible over long times, but statistical averages are. Choosing the time step The step must resolve the fastest motion in the system, roughly one-tenth to one-twentieth of the shortest vibrational period. Metals with embedded-atom potentials commonly use 1 to 5 fs; systems with light atoms (hydrogen) or stiff bonds need 0.5 fs or less. The practical test is to run in the NVE ensemble and check that the total energy is conserved: a steady drift or large fluctuations mean the step is too long, the cutoff is too abrupt or the potential is not smooth. Each step is 10 −15 s, so a nanosecond needs 10 6 steps and a microsecond 10 9 ."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"3. Interatomic potentials","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#potentials","x":"The potential is a model of the Born–Oppenheimer energy surface (see DFT ). The faster the potential, the simpler its functional form and the more physics it leaves out. The simplest, the Lennard-Jones pair potential, is a good model for noble gases and a useful teaching and testing tool: U(r) = 4ε [ (σ/r) 12 − (σ/r) 6 ], minimum at r = 2 1/6 σ, depth ε This interactive explorer needs JavaScript to run. Class Idea Used for Cautions Pair (Lennard-Jones, Morse, Buckingham) Energy is a sum over pairs Noble gases, simple ionic and model systems Cannot reproduce metals’ elastic anisotropy (forces C 12 = C 44 , the Cauchy relation) Embedded atom (EAM) and MEAM E = Σ i F i (ρ i ) + ½Σ ij φ(r ij ), where ρ i is a sum of neighbour contributions; MEAM adds angular dependence fcc, bcc and hcp metals and alloys; defects, surfaces, plasticity Fitted to bulk properties; check stacking-fault energy, surface energy, melting point Bond-order and covalent (Tersoff, Stillinger–Weber, AIREBO) Bond strength depends on local coordination Si, Ge, C, SiC, BN Limited transferability between phases Reactive (ReaxFF, COMB) Bond order determined dynamically; charges equilibrated Oxidation, combustion, catalysis Slow; each parameter set is for a defined chemistry Charged systems (Coulomb + short-range) Long-range electrostatics (Ewald, PPPM) plus repulsion Oxides, salts, ceramics Long-range sums are expensive; need charge neutrality Machine-learned (GAP, MTP, ACE, SNAP, neural-network) Flexible functions of local atomic environments, fitted to DFT energies, forces and stresses Complex chemistry at near-DFT accuracy Valid only inside the training data; 10 to 1000 times slower than EAM Universal / foundation models (for example M3GNet, CHGNet, MACE-MP) Machine-learned potentials trained on very large DFT databases across the periodic table Quick screening, initial exploration Systematic softening of energy surfaces; validate before relying Validating a potential A potential is a model that must pass tests for the property you want . A minimal checklist, compared with experiment or DFT: lattice parameter and cohesive energy of the relevant structures; elastic constants and phonon spectrum; point-defect formation energies and migration barriers; surface energies, stacking-fault energies, grain-boundary energies; melting temperature, thermal expansion; relative energies of competing phases ("},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"4. Neighbour lists and cost","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#efficiency","x":"Short-range potentials are truncated at a cutoff r c , so each atom interacts with the neighbours within it. Checking every pair would scale as N 2 . Instead a neighbour list (Verlet list) records atoms within r c plus a skin distance and is rebuilt only every few steps, using spatial cell lists to find candidates quickly; the cost per step then scales as N . Large runs divide space into domains handled by different processors (domain decomposition, with MPI) and exchange atoms near the domain edges, so billions of atoms are feasible on supercomputers. Long-range Coulomb forces use Ewald summation or particle-mesh methods (PPPM, PME), scaling as N log N ."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"5. Controlling temperature and pressure","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#control","x":"With no control the simulation conserves energy (NVE). To model an experiment at constant temperature or pressure, a thermostat or barostat is added. The algorithms differ in whether they produce the correct ensemble. Method Controls Correct ensemble? Note Velocity rescaling T No Simple; used for quick equilibration only (the animation above uses it to ramp temperature) Berendsen T, p No (suppresses fluctuations) Robust and fast to equilibrate; not for production runs that need fluctuations Nosé–Hoover (chains) T Yes, canonical Standard choice; deterministic; the damping time sets coupling Stochastic velocity rescaling (CSVR / Bussi) T Yes, canonical Efficient equilibration and correct fluctuations Langevin T Yes, canonical Adds friction and random forces; perturbs dynamics, so not for diffusion Parrinello–Rahman, Martyna–Tobias–Klein p (cell shape and size) Yes, isothermal–isobaric Allows shear and shape change, needed for solid–solid transformations Thermostat strength matters: strong coupling distorts the natural dynamics, so for transport coefficients the system is equilibrated with a thermostat and then run in NVE."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"6. Workflow","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#workflow","x":"Build the structure and cell (see crystal structures and periodic cells ), with box edges at least twice the cutoff. Minimise the energy (conjugate gradient or FIRE) to remove overlaps and high forces before dynamics begin. Assign velocities from the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution at the target temperature, remove net momentum. Equilibrate , usually NVT then NPT, until energy, temperature, pressure and density stop drifting. Produce the data run in the chosen ensemble, saving configurations and properties at intervals longer than a few vibrational periods. Analyse and estimate uncertainties from independent runs or block averages."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"7. Analysis","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#analysis","x":"Structure. Radial distribution function g ( r ) and coordination numbers; common neighbour analysis or polyhedral template matching labels each atom as fcc, bcc, hcp or other, revealing stacking faults, twins and grain boundaries; the centrosymmetry parameter highlights defects. Defects. The dislocation extraction algorithm (DXA in OVITO) finds dislocation lines and Burgers vectors; Wigner–Seitz analysis counts vacancies and interstitials after radiation damage. Transport. Diffusion from the slope of the mean-squared displacement, MSD = 6 Dt in three dimensions, using unwrapped coordinates (details in diffusion and kinetics ); viscosity and thermal conductivity from Green–Kubo integrals or non-equilibrium set-ups. Mechanics. The stress tensor from the virial; stress–strain curves from imposed deformation; elastic constants from small strains or fluctuations. Thermodynamics. Melting temperature by two-phase coexistence (solid and liquid in one box: the interface advances toward melting or freezing depending on temperature); free energies by thermodynamic integration."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"8. Applications in materials","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#applications","x":"MD is strongest where atomic mechanisms control behaviour on nanosecond time scales: tensile and compression tests of nanowires and nanopillars; nanoindentation; dislocation nucleation and motion at interfaces; grain-boundary sliding and migration; dislocation–precipitate and dislocation–solute interaction; crack propagation and brittle–ductile behaviour at a crack tip; shock compression; radiation-damage cascades; melting, solidification and nucleation of simple systems; diffusion in liquids and fast-diffusion solids; thermal conductivity of nanostructures."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"9. Limits","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#limits","x":"Time scale. Nanoseconds to microseconds. Processes governed by rare events with barriers much above k B T are not reached directly. Strain rate. Deformation is typically driven at 10 7 to 10 10 s −1 , some ten orders of magnitude faster than a tensile test, and flow stresses are correspondingly high. Rate effects must be considered before comparing with experiment. Length scale. Millions to billions of atoms span tens to hundreds of nanometres, far below a grain in an engineering alloy, so surfaces and boundary effects are enormous. Potential accuracy. The result cannot be better than the energy surface supplied. Electronic effects (magnetism, charge transfer) are absent unless built in. Classical nuclei. Quantum effects (zero-point energy, tunnelling of hydrogen) are neglected, and classical heat capacity is wrong at low temperature."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"10. A minimal input","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#lammps","x":"LAMMPS is the most widely used open-source MD code. This input equilibrates a 4000-atom copper crystal at 300 K and zero pressure with an EAM potential (the potential file ships with the program): units metal boundary p p p lattice fcc 3.615 region box block 0 10 0 10 0 10 create_box 1 box create_atoms 1 box mass 1 63.546 pair_style eam pair_coeff * * Cu_u3.eam velocity all create 300.0 12345 mom yes rot no fix 1 all npt temp 300.0 300.0 0.1 iso 0.0 0.0 1.0 timestep 0.002 # 2 fs in metal units (time in ps) thermo 100 run 10000 In LAMMPS “metal” units, distance is in Å, energy in eV, time in ps and pressure in bar. The numbers 0.1 and 1.0 on the npt line are the temperature and pressure damping times in ps. Key takeaways MD integrates Newton's equations with velocity-Verlet. Use a time step of about a tenth to a twentieth of the shortest vibrational period and check energy conservation in NVE. The potential defines the accuracy, so validate it on the property you need. Use canonical thermostats and proper barostats (Nose-Hoover, stochastic velocity rescaling, Parrinello-Rahman) for production runs. The workflow is build, minimise, equilibrate, produce and analyse, with error bars from independent runs. Limits: nanoseconds to microseconds, very high strain rates, classical nuclei and no electrons."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"11. Check your understanding","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#check","x":"A NVE run shows the total energy rising steadily. List three things to check. (1) The time step may be too large for the fastest vibration. (2) The potential or its cutoff may be discontinuous, so the force jumps at r c (use a smoothly truncated or shifted form). (3) A neighbour-list skin that is too small can cause missed interactions when atoms move; also check for overlapping atoms in the starting structure. Why can one not use Berendsen coupling for a calculation of heat capacity from energy fluctuations? Berendsen coupling damps the energy fluctuations and does not generate the canonical distribution. The fluctuation formula for C V relies on the true canonical variance of the energy, so a canonical thermostat (Nosé–Hoover chains or stochastic velocity rescaling) is needed. A simulated tensile test gives a yield stress ten times the experimental value. Is the potential necessarily wrong? No. Strain rates in MD are far higher than in experiments, and nanoscale samples have few pre-existing dislocation sources, so high stresses are expected. Compare like with like (rate, size, defect content) and check whether the potential reproduces stacking-fault and surface energies, which control the mechanism."},{"n":6,"t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"12. References and further reading","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#refs","x":"Allen, M. P. and Tildesley, D. J., Computer Simulation of Liquids , Oxford University Press. Frenkel, D. and Smit, B., Understanding Molecular Simulation , Academic Press. Verlet, L., Computer “experiments” on classical fluids. I., Phys. Rev. 159, 98 (1967). Daw, M. S. and Baskes, M. I., Embedded-atom method, Phys. Rev. B 29, 6443 (1984). Hoover, W. G., Canonical dynamics: equilibrium phase-space distributions, Phys. Rev. A 31, 1695 (1985). Parrinello, M. and Rahman, A., Polymorphic transitions in single crystals, J. Appl. Phys. 52, 7182 (1981). Bussi, G., Donadio, D. and Parrinello, M., Canonical sampling through velocity rescaling, J. Chem. Phys. 126, 014101 (2007). Behler, J. and Parrinello, M., Generalized neural-network representation of high-dimensional potential-energy surfaces, Phys. Rev. Lett. 98, 146401 (2007). Bartók, A. P. et al., Gaussian approximation potentials, Phys. Rev. Lett. 104, 136403 (2010). Thompson, A. P. et al., LAMMPS, Comput. Phys. Commun. 271, 108171 (2022). Stukowski, A., Visualization and analysis of atomistic simulation data with OVITO, Modelling Simul. Mater. Sci. Eng. 18, 015012 (2010). Previous topic Density functional theory in practice Next topic Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo"},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html","x":"Topics / Atoms Topic 7 Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo Where molecular dynamics follows real motion, Monte Carlo (MC) takes a shortcut: it proposes random changes and keeps or rejects them with rules that guarantee the right statistics. Because moves need not be physical, MC reaches equilibrium states that dynamics would take far too long to find, and its kinetic variant reaches seconds and beyond. Reading time about 12 min Builds on 3. Statistical mechanics Leads to — Category Atoms After reading this page you should be able to: Explain importance sampling, detailed balance and the Metropolis acceptance rule. Set up a lattice-model MC simulation and choose suitable moves. Describe cluster expansions and how they link DFT to alloy thermodynamics. Explain how kinetic Monte Carlo advances physical time, and what it needs as input."},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"1. Why sample randomly","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#why","x":"An equilibrium average in the canonical ensemble is an integral over all configurations: ⟨A⟩ = ∫ A(x) exp[−U(x)/k B T] dx / ∫ exp[−U(x)/k B T] dx The space is enormously high-dimensional (3 N coordinates, or 2 N states for N two-state sites), so grids are impossible. Sampling configurations uniformly at random fails as well: nearly all random configurations (atoms overlapping, or a random jumble of A and B) have very high energy and negligible Boltzmann weight, so the average is dominated by rare lucky samples. Importance sampling draws configurations with probability proportional to their Boltzmann weight, so that the average is simply the mean of the samples, ⟨ A ⟩ ≈ (1/ M )Σ m A ( x m ). The samples are generated by a Markov chain : each new configuration is produced from the current one by a random rule. If the rule satisfies detailed balance , so that the flow from state a to b equals the flow back at equilibrium, π(a) P(a→b) = π(b) P(b→a), π(a) ∝ exp[−U(a)/k B T] (and the chain can reach every state, which is ergodicity), then the chain converges to the Boltzmann distribution."},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"2. Metropolis algorithm","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#metropolis","x":"The simplest rule satisfying detailed balance is due to Metropolis and co-workers (1953). Given a symmetric proposal (the probability of proposing a → b equals that of b → a ): Pick a trial move (for example, flip one spin). Compute the energy change Δ E = E new − E old . If Δ E ≤ 0, accept. Otherwise accept with probability exp(−Δ E / k B T ). If rejected, keep the old configuration (and count it again in the averages). P acc (a→b) = min[ 1, exp(−ΔE / k B T) ] Hastings (1970) generalised this to asymmetric proposals by multiplying by the ratio of proposal probabilities. Two practical points: moves that change energy locally can be evaluated cheaply, by recomputing only the affected neighbours, so each step costs O (1) on a lattice; and the acceptance rate should be tuned (often 20 to 50%) by adjusting step sizes, because very high acceptance means moves are too small and very low means they are too large. One sweep is N attempted moves, and sweeps are the natural unit of MC “time”."},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"3. The Ising model","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#ising","x":"The Ising model places a spin s i = ±1 on each lattice site, with energy E = −J Σ ⟨ij⟩ s i s j − h Σ i s i summed over nearest-neighbour pairs. For J > 0 neighbours prefer to align. It is the minimal model of a phase transition: on the two-dimensional square lattice it has an exact solution (Onsager, 1944) with critical temperature k B T c = 2 J /ln(1 + √2) ≈ 2.269 J . Below T c a spontaneous magnetisation appears; above it, thermal disorder wins. The model maps directly to binary alloys and lattice gases, where the spin is the species on a site (A or B, or atom and vacancy) and J is built from pair energies; its ordering transition is the same mathematics as ordering in β-brass. Metropolis Monte Carlo on a 64×64 Ising lattice at T = 1.6 (critical temperature 2.27, in units of J /k B ), starting from a random arrangement. Domains of each orientation form and coarsen; the sweep count is shown in the title. This interactive simulation needs JavaScript to run. Near T c the correlation length diverges and Metropolis single-spin flips suffer critical slowing down : large domains change very slowly. Cluster algorithms (Swendsen–Wang, Wolff) flip whole correlated clusters and remove most of the problem for such models."},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"4. Moves for materials","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#moves","x":"The power of MC for materials is the freedom to design moves. Any set of moves that satisfies detailed balance and reaches the whole state space is allowed, whether or not it resembles real dynamics. Move Ensemble What it does Used for Displacement NVT Shifts one atom by a small random vector Off-lattice equilibrium; relaxation Exchange (swap) NVT with fixed composition Swaps the species of two atoms, even far apart Chemical ordering and segregation in alloys, where diffusion in MD is far too slow Volume change NPT Rescales the cell; acceptance includes the p Δ V and − Nk B T ln( V ′/ V ) terms Thermal expansion, equations of state Insertion / deletion Grand canonical (μVT) Adds or removes an atom against a reservoir Adsorption, gas uptake, vacancy concentrations Species change Semi-grand canonical Changes the identity of a site at fixed chemical-potential difference Δμ Composition as a function of Δμ, phase boundaries of alloys Hybrid MC/MD NVT or NPT Alternates MD steps (relaxing local strain) with swap moves Segregation to grain boundaries, ordering in multicomponent alloys with realistic potentials"},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"5. Lattice models and cluster expansion","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#lattice","x":"Real alloys are not Ising models, but their configurational energy can be mapped onto one. A cluster expansion (Sanchez, Ducastelle and Gratias, 1984) writes the energy of any arrangement σ of species on a fixed parent lattice as a sum over clusters of sites (single sites, pairs, triplets, …): E(σ) = Σ α m α J α ⟨Φ α (σ)⟩ where Φ α are products of site variables over cluster α, m α is a multiplicity and J α are the effective cluster interactions (ECIs). The ECIs are fitted to a set of DFT energies of ordered structures (typically tens to hundreds of them), and the fit is checked by cross-validation. The expansion is exact if all clusters are included; in practice it is truncated, and the truncation error determines accuracy. Once fitted, the energy of any configuration costs a few operations, so MC can simulate millions of atoms over many sweeps with ab initio energetics. Typical outputs are order–disorder transition temperatures, solvus lines, ground-state structures, short-range order and its temperature dependence, and (with vibrational and electronic free energies added) first-principles phase diagrams. Software packages include ATAT, CASM and icet. The same framework handles interstitial ordering (carbon in iron), vacancies and magnetic degrees of freedom by adding more site variables."},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"6. Better sampling","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#advanced","x":"Parallel tempering (replica exchange). Several copies run at different temperatures and periodically swap configurations with acceptance min[1, exp((β i − β j )( E i − E j ))]. High-temperature replicas cross barriers, low-temperature replicas refine; the combination escapes metastable states. Flat-histogram methods (Wang–Landau, multicanonical). Estimate the density of states g ( E ) by a random walk that visits all energies equally. From g ( E ), all thermodynamic quantities at all temperatures follow from one run, including free energies and latent heats. Cluster moves for critical systems, as noted above. Biased moves that propose chemically sensible changes more often, corrected by Hastings ratios."},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"7. Kinetic Monte Carlo","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#kmc","x":"Metropolis MC samples equilibrium but its “time” is not physical. Kinetic Monte Carlo (kMC) instead chooses real events with real rates and advances a physical clock. It rests on the picture of a system that sits in a potential-energy basin, vibrates for a long time and then hops (a diffusive jump, a reaction, a dislocation depinning) to another basin; the hops are rare, independent, random events (a Poisson process). Rather than simulating the vibrations, kMC jumps straight from hop to hop. Each possible event i has a rate, typically from harmonic transition-state theory: k i = ν i exp( −E a,i / k B T ) where E a, i is the activation barrier and ν i the attempt frequency (about 10 12 –10 13 s −1 , from vibrational frequencies). The rejection-free algorithm (Bortz–Kalos–Lebowitz; also called the residence-time or Gillespie algorithm) is: List all possible events and compute the rates k i and the total rate R = Σ i k i . Choose event i with probability k i / R , by drawing a uniform random number and finding where it falls on the cumulative sum of rates. Execute it (move the atom, change the state). Advance the clock by Δ t = −ln( u )/ R , with u a second uniform random number in (0, 1). Update the event list (only events near the change are affected) and repeat. Every step makes progress, so the method is efficient even when rates are very small, and the time scale can reach seconds, hours or longer; the speed is limited by the fastest events, since the clock moves in steps of 1/ R . If some events are much faster than others (for example back-and-forth hops between two sites), they dominate the step count without producing net change, and special treatment (coarse-graining, superbasins) is needed. Input is the bottleneck kMC needs a catalogue of events and their barriers. On a rigid lattice the barriers can be tabulated from DFT nudged-elastic-band calculations, but they depend on the local atomic environment (neighbouring solutes, strain), which leads to either many barriers or a barrier model fitted as a function of environment. Off-lattice (adaptive) kMC finds events on the fly by searching the potential-energy surface from each new minimum, at much higher cost per step but without having to guess the events beforehand. Typical uses Vacancy-mediated diffusion and clustering of solutes in alloys; precipitate nucleation and coarsening; irradiation-damage e"},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"8. Grain growth with the Potts model","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#potts","x":"The Potts model generalises Ising to Q states: each site carries an integer (a grain orientation) and neighbouring sites of different orientation cost energy J . Domains of equal integer represent grains, and the boundary between them has an energy proportional to its length. Metropolis dynamics reduces the boundary energy, so grains grow by boundary migration. Studies by Anderson, Srolovitz and co-workers (1984) used this to study normal grain growth, where the classical parabolic law predicts mean size ∝ t 1/2 , and to examine the grain-size distribution; fitted growth exponents in these models are usually somewhat below one half. The relationship to physical time must be calibrated, boundary mobility and energy are simple functions of misorientation in the basic model, and anisotropy or particle pinning require extensions; the model is nevertheless a popular tool for exploring grain growth and recrystallisation topologically."},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"9. MC compared with MD","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#compare","x":"Molecular dynamics Metropolis Monte Carlo Kinetic Monte Carlo Output Trajectory with real dynamics Equilibrium samples Evolution with physical time Time Femtosecond steps, up to microseconds Sweeps; not physical time Physical, up to seconds and beyond Moves Dictated by forces Any move satisfying detailed balance List of real events with rates Needs Forces (potential) Energies only Event catalogue and barriers Best for Mechanisms, short-time dynamics, transport Ordering, segregation, phase equilibrium Slow diffusion-controlled evolution"},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"10. A minimal code","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#code","x":"A complete Metropolis sweep for the 2D Ising model, in units where J = k B = 1, is only a few lines of Python: import numpy as np rng = np.random.default_rng(1) N, T = 32, 1.6 s = rng.choice([-1, 1], size=(N, N)) def sweep(s, T): for _ in range(N * N): i, j = rng.integers(N, size=2) nb = s[(i+1) % N, j] + s[(i-1) % N, j] + s[i, (j+1) % N] + s[i, (j-1) % N] dE = 2 * s[i, j] * nb if dE <= 0 or rng.random() < np.exp(-dE / T): s[i, j] *= -1 for _ in range(500): sweep(s, T) print(\"magnetisation per spin:\", s.mean()) Below T c the magnetisation per spin ends near ±1; above it, it fluctuates near zero. Try T = 3 and compare; then plot energy against sweeps to see equilibration. Key takeaways Importance sampling with detailed balance produces Boltzmann-distributed samples; the Metropolis rule accepts with probability min(1, exp(-dE/kT)). Moves need not be physical: swap moves equilibrate chemical order far faster than dynamics can. Lattice models and cluster expansions carry DFT energetics into alloy thermodynamics at large size. Kinetic Monte Carlo advances physical time, dt = -ln(u)/R, using rates built from barriers. kMC is only as good as its event catalogue and barriers."},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"11. Check your understanding","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#check","x":"Why is a rejected move still counted in the averages? Because the Markov chain must stay in state a with the probability 1 − Σ b P ( a → b ) for detailed balance to hold. Repeated states carry weight, which is how low-probability states are visited less often. Dropping rejected steps would bias the average toward high-energy states. A kMC run has rates summing to R = 10 6 s −1 . About how much physical time does one step advance? On average Δ t = 1/ R = 10 −6 s (the expectation of −ln( u )/ R is 1/ R ). A million steps then represent about one second. Why does MC with swap moves equilibrate a ternary alloy much faster than MD? In MD, atoms must diffuse to rearrange, which at ordinary temperatures takes times far beyond what MD can reach. A swap move exchanges two atoms directly regardless of distance and barrier, so the arrangement can sample the equilibrium distribution of chemical order in a small number of sweeps."},{"n":7,"t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"12. References and further reading","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#refs","x":"Landau, D. P. and Binder, K., A Guide to Monte Carlo Simulations in Statistical Physics , Cambridge University Press. Newman, M. E. J. and Barkema, G. T., Monte Carlo Methods in Statistical Physics , Oxford University Press (1999). Metropolis, N. et al., Equation of state calculations by fast computing machines, J. Chem. Phys. 21, 1087 (1953). Hastings, W. K., Monte Carlo sampling methods using Markov chains and their applications, Biometrika 57, 97 (1970). Swendsen, R. H. and Wang, J.-S., Nonuniversal critical dynamics in Monte Carlo simulations, Phys. Rev. Lett. 58, 86 (1987); Wolff, U., Collective Monte Carlo updating for spin systems, Phys. Rev. Lett. 62, 361 (1989). Wang, F. and Landau, D. P., Efficient, multiple-range random walk algorithm to calculate the density of states, Phys. Rev. Lett. 86, 2050 (2001). Sanchez, J. M., Ducastelle, F. and Gratias, D., Generalized cluster description of multicomponent systems, Physica A 128, 334 (1984). Bortz, A. B., Kalos, M. H. and Lebowitz, J. L., A new algorithm for Monte Carlo simulation of Ising spin systems, J. Comput. Phys. 17, 10 (1975). Gillespie, D. T., Exact stochastic simulation of coupled chemical reactions, J. Phys. Chem. 81, 2340 (1977). Voter, A. F., Introduction to the kinetic Monte Carlo method, in Radiation Effects in Solids , Springer (2007). Anderson, M. P., Srolovitz, D. J., Grest, G. S. and Sahni, P. S., Computer simulation of grain growth, Acta Metall. 32, 783 (1984). Previous topic Molecular dynamics Next topic Diffusion and kinetics"},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"Diffusion and kinetics","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html","x":"Topics / Kinetics Topic 8 Diffusion and kinetics Thermodynamics says where a material is heading; diffusion usually decides how fast it gets there. Homogenisation, carburising, precipitation, oxidation, sintering, creep and grain growth are all limited by how quickly atoms move. Reading time about 13 min Builds on 4. Thermodynamics and CALPHAD Leads to 9. Phase-field 10. Finite elements Category Kinetics After reading this page you should be able to: Write Fick’s laws and use the error-function and Gaussian solutions to estimate diffusion distances. Explain why the real driving force is a chemical-potential gradient, and when diffusion runs uphill. Relate the diffusion coefficient to atomistic jump mechanisms and the Arrhenius law. Outline how D is obtained by DFT, MD and kinetic Monte Carlo, and solve the diffusion equation with a stable finite-difference scheme."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"1. Fick’s laws","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#fick","x":"Fick’s first law says that the flux J of atoms (amount per area per time) is proportional to the concentration gradient and directed down it: J = −D ∇c The proportionality constant D is the diffusion coefficient (m² s −1 ). Combining this with conservation of matter gives Fick’s second law , the diffusion equation: ∂c/∂t = ∇ · ( D ∇c ) → ∂c/∂t = D ∂²c/∂x² (constant D, one dimension) Two closed-form solutions cover many practical problems. For a diffusion couple or a surface held at fixed concentration, the solution involves the error function; for a thin film of total amount M per unit area spreading into a large body, it is a Gaussian: Couple (step initial profile): c(x,t) = ½ (c 1 + c 2 ) − ½ (c 1 − c 2 ) erf( x / 2√(Dt) ) Surface at c s (carburising): c(x,t) = c s − (c s − c 0 ) erf( x / 2√(Dt) ) Thin film: c(x,t) = M / √(4πDt) · exp( −x² / 4Dt ) A diffusion couple: a sharp step smears out, with the width growing as √(Dt). The curve is the error-function solution with the time shown by the bar below. All three depend on x /√( Dt ), so the affected distance grows as the square root of time , and a rule of thumb is the diffusion length ≈ 2√( Dt ) (or √( Dt )). Doubling the distance takes four times as long. This is why the time to homogenise a segregated structure scales with the square of the segregation spacing: halving the dendrite arm spacing cuts the required time by four. Worked example. Take carbon in ferrite with illustrative values D 0 = 2×10 −6 m² s −1 and Q = 84 kJ mol −1 at 700 °C (973 K): D = 2×10 −6 exp[−84 000/(8.314 × 973)] ≈ 6×10 −11 m² s −1 . After one hour, √( Dt ) ≈ 0.5 mm: carbon moves far in a thin sheet, while a substitutional element such as manganese, with a diffusivity many orders of magnitude lower at the same temperature, barely moves on the scale of a micrometre."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"2. The true driving force","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#force","x":"Fick’s first law with a constant D is an approximation. Atoms move to lower their free energy, so the driving force is the gradient of the chemical potential , not of concentration: J i = − M i c i ∇μ i → D i = M i k B T · Θ, Θ = ∂ln a i / ∂ln c i where M i is the atomic mobility and Θ the thermodynamic factor . In an ideal solution Θ = 1 and Fick’s law holds in its simple form. If unlike atoms attract, Θ > 1 and diffusion is faster than the intrinsic atomic mobility suggests; if like atoms attract so strongly that the Gibbs energy curve has negative curvature (inside the spinodal; see CALPHAD ), Θ < 0 and the effective D is negative: atoms diffuse uphill against the concentration gradient, which is spinodal decomposition. Uphill diffusion also occurs in alloys of multiple elements even outside a miscibility gap: in Darken’s classic experiment, carbon moved out of a high-silicon steel into a plain steel that started with the same carbon content, because silicon raises the chemical potential of carbon. Two practical consequences. First, mobilities are the fundamental kinetic quantity, and CALPHAD-style databases store them as functions of composition and temperature next to the thermodynamics. Second, in a binary substitutional couple the interdiffusion coefficient combines the two intrinsic mobilities (Darken): D̃ = ( x B D* A + x A D* B ) Θ The two species typically have different tracer diffusivities D *, so a net flow of vacancies accompanies the net atomic flux; inert markers placed at the original interface then shift relative to the ends of the specimen. This is the Kirkendall effect , and it can leave pores (Kirkendall voids) on the fast-diffusing side."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"3. Atomistic mechanisms","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#mechanisms","x":"An atom in a crystal sits in a potential well and occasionally gains enough thermal energy to jump to a neighbouring site. The pathway depends on the type of atom and the defects present: Vacancy mechanism. A substitutional atom swaps with a neighbouring vacancy. It is the dominant route for self-diffusion and for substitutional solutes (Mn, Cr, Ni in iron; Cu in aluminium). Atoms can only move when a vacancy is next to them, so the rate involves both the vacancy concentration and the jump rate. Interstitial mechanism. Small solutes (C, N, H, O) hop between interstitial sites without needing a vacancy. These jumps are fast, with low activation energy, which is why carbon equilibrates in steel in minutes while alloying elements do not. Short-circuit paths. Grain boundaries, dislocation cores and free surfaces have an open structure and diffusivities that can be many orders of magnitude higher than in the lattice, dominating at low temperature and in fine-grained or heavily deformed material. Others. Interstitialcy (a displaced pair), ring and exchange mechanisms matter in some systems, for example in covalent semiconductors. The vacancy mechanism. The dashed ring is the vacancy; the highlighted atom is displaced each time the vacancy passes it. The hop sequence in the animation is illustrative and is replayed in reverse to loop."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"4. Temperature dependence","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#arrhenius","x":"A hop requires crossing an energy barrier E m (the migration energy). The jump rate is thermally activated, and the diffusion coefficient follows an Arrhenius law: D = D 0 exp( −Q / RT ) Plotting ln D against 1/ T gives a straight line of slope − Q / R , and a change of slope signals a change of mechanism (for example lattice to grain-boundary diffusion). The microscopic content comes from the random-walk theory: for jumps of length λ at total rate Γ, D = Γλ²/6 in three dimensions. For vacancy diffusion in a cubic crystal this becomes D = f a² ν exp( −(E f + E m ) / k B T ) (fcc; vibrational entropy factors omitted) where f is the correlation factor (about 0.78 for fcc and 0.73 for bcc, since a tracer atom is slightly more likely to jump back into the vacancy it just left than elsewhere), a the lattice parameter, ν an attempt frequency, and the activation energy of self-diffusion is Q = E f + E m , vacancy formation plus migration. For interstitial diffusion, Q = E m only, because the available interstitial sites are always there. A useful sanity check is the empirical rule that Q for vacancy self-diffusion in close-packed metals is about 17 to 18 RT m , where T m is the melting temperature. For copper ( T m = 1358 K) this gives about 200 kJ mol −1 , close to the measured value of roughly 200 kJ mol −1 . It means that diffusion at a given fraction of the melting temperature (the “homologous temperature”) is similar across many metals."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"5. Computing D","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#compute","x":"Several complementary routes exist, matched to how fast the species moves. Method What it gives Strength Limit Molecular dynamics D from the mean-squared displacement, D = lim MSD/(6 t ); or from the velocity autocorrelation integral Direct, includes all anharmonicity; ideal for liquids, fast ionic conductors, grain boundaries at high T Only practical for D above roughly 10 −11 m² s −1 within nanoseconds, so lattice diffusion in solids is out of reach well below the melting point DFT + transition-state theory E f , E m (nudged elastic band), attempt frequency ν from phonons (Vineyard), then D by the formula above Parameter-free; works for any T ; resolves each solute–vacancy interaction Needs many calculations; assumes harmonic transition-state theory and a defined mechanism Five-frequency model (Lidiard–LeClaire) Solute diffusion and vacancy drag in dilute fcc alloys from five jump frequencies computed by DFT Compact route from DFT to solute D and the Onsager coefficients Dilute limit; fcc geometry (other lattices need extensions) Kinetic Monte Carlo D from the MSD of tracer atoms in a lattice with environment-dependent barriers Reaches physical time for slow diffusion; handles concentrated alloys and correlations Needs the barrier model (see Monte Carlo ) Databases and assessment Mobility parameters fitted to experiments Directly usable in diffusion codes with thermodynamic data Only for systems already assessed Whichever route is used, results should be compared with experiments at one or more temperatures. Agreement within a factor of two in D is considered good, because a 0.1 eV error in the barrier changes D by a factor of about 3 at 1000 K and by about 10 at 500 K."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"6. Solving the equation numerically","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#numerics","x":"When D depends on composition, when several phases are present, or when the geometry is complicated, there is no closed-form solution and the equation is solved on a grid. For one dimension with spacing Δ x and time step Δ t , the simplest scheme is the explicit (forward-time, centred-space) method : c i n+1 = c i n + r ( c i+1 n − 2c i n + c i−1 n ), r = D Δt / Δx² It is stable only if r ≤ ½ (and r ≤ 1/4 in two dimensions, 1/6 in three); exceeding it makes errors grow exponentially and the solution oscillate. Refining the grid by a factor of two forces the time step down by four, so explicit schemes become expensive. Implicit schemes (backward Euler) are stable for any step; they require solving a tridiagonal linear system at each step, which is cheap in one dimension. The Crank–Nicolson average of the two is second-order accurate in time. For conserving the total amount of solute, which matters in diffusion couples, a finite-volume formulation (fluxes across cell faces) is preferred. Boundary and interface conditions Fixed concentration (Dirichlet): a surface in equilibrium with a carburising atmosphere. Zero flux (Neumann): a symmetry plane or an impermeable wall. Mass-transfer (Robin): the flux at the surface is J = β( c gas − c surface ), with a finite transfer coefficient β. Moving interface between phases α and β: concentrations on either side are set by local equilibrium (from thermodynamics) and the interface velocity v follows from solute conservation, ( c β − c α ) v = J α − J β . This Stefan-type problem is the basis of diffusion-controlled transformation models in steels. An explicit solver in Python, with the surface held at c = 1 and zero flux at the far end, fits in a few lines. The time step is chosen with r = 0.2 for safety: import numpy as np D, L, nx = 1e-11, 1e-3, 201 # m^2/s, m, grid points dx = L / (nx - 1) dt = 0.2 * dx**2 / D # r = D*dt/dx^2 = 0.2 (stable: r <= 0.5) c = np.zeros(nx); c[0] = 1.0 # surface at c = 1, initially empty t = 0.0 while t < 3600: # one hour c[1:-1] += D * dt / dx**2 * (c[2:] - 2*c[1:-1] + c[:-2]) c[0], c[-1] = 1.0, c[-2] # fixed surface, zero flux at the far end t += dt Compare the result with the error-function solution c = 1 − erf( x /2√( Dt )) as a first verification, then change r to 0.6 and watch the solution blow up. This interactive solver needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"7. Multicomponent diffusion","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#multi","x":"In an alloy with several solutes, the flux of each element depends on the gradient of all the others: J i = − Σ j D ij ∇c j The full D ij matrix has off-diagonal terms (cross-diffusion) of either sign, and Onsager reciprocity constrains it. Practically, one works with chemical potentials and mobilities, J i = −Σ j L ij ∇μ j , with mobilities from a database and thermodynamics from CALPHAD, so cross effects (like carbon moving in a silicon gradient) come out automatically. The difference between interstitial and substitutional diffusivities, often six or more orders of magnitude, makes the equations stiff and motivates special treatments such as paraequilibrium, where carbon redistributes while substitutional elements are frozen."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"8. From diffusion to microstructure","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#micro","x":"Diffusion enters models of microstructure change in a few standard ways. Nucleation. Classical nucleation theory gives the barrier for a spherical nucleus, Δ G * = 16πγ³/(3Δ G v ²), where γ is the interface energy and Δ G v the volume driving force (from CALPHAD, less any strain energy); the rate is proportional to exp(−Δ G */ k B T ) times an atom-attachment term controlled by diffusion. Growth. Diffusion-controlled growth of a particle or plate is parabolic in time: thickness or radius ∝ √( Dt ), with a proportionality set by the supersaturation. Coarsening. Small precipitates dissolve and large ones grow because of the Gibbs–Thomson effect (higher solubility around curved surfaces). The Lifshitz–Slyozov–Wagner (LSW) theory gives r 3 − r 0 3 = Kt , with K proportional to D , γ and the equilibrium solubility, which sets how long strengthening precipitates stay fine at service temperature. Overall transformation kinetics. The Johnson–Mehl–Avrami–Kolmogorov equation, f = 1 − exp(− kt n ), combines nucleation and growth into a sigmoidal fraction-transformed curve; the exponent n hints at the mechanism. Models that resolve the microstructure itself, such as phase-field , solve diffusion and interface motion together. Key takeaways Diffusion distance grows as the square root of Dt, so the time needed scales with the square of the length. The real driving force is the chemical-potential gradient; diffusion can run uphill, and Darken and Kirkendall effects follow from unequal mobilities. D = D0 exp(-Q/RT). For vacancy diffusion Q is formation plus migration energy; for interstitials it is the migration energy alone. D can come from MD (fast species), DFT with transition-state theory, or kinetic Monte Carlo, and should be checked against experiment. An explicit finite-difference scheme is stable only for r = D dt/dx squared of at most one half in one dimension."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"9. Check your understanding","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#check","x":"A homogenisation anneal takes 10 hours for segregation with a 100 µm spacing. How long for a 50 µm spacing at the same temperature? The time scales as the square of the length ( t ∝ x ²/ D ), so halving the spacing needs one quarter of the time: 2.5 hours. Why is the activation energy of vacancy self-diffusion larger than the migration energy alone? An atom can only move if a vacancy is adjacent, and vacancies must be created thermally at an energy cost E f . The equilibrium vacancy fraction contributes a factor exp(− E f / k B T ), so the total activation energy is E f + E m . Your explicit diffusion solver gives wild oscillations. What do you check first? The stability number r = D Δ t /Δ x ². It must be at most 0.5 in one dimension. Reduce Δ t (or coarsen Δ x ), or switch to an implicit scheme."},{"n":8,"t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"10. References and further reading","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#refs","x":"Crank, J., The Mathematics of Diffusion , Oxford University Press (1975). Shewmon, P., Diffusion in Solids , Springer. Mehrer, H., Diffusion in Solids: Fundamentals, Methods, Materials, Diffusion-Controlled Processes , Springer (2007). Balluffi, R. W., Allen, S. M. and Carter, W. C., Kinetics of Materials , Wiley (2005). Porter, D. A., Easterling, K. E. and Sherif, M. Y., Phase Transformations in Metals and Alloys , CRC Press. Darken, L. S., Diffusion, mobility and their interrelation through free energy in binary metallic systems, Trans. AIME 175, 184 (1948); Darken, L. S., Diffusion of carbon in austenite with a discontinuity in composition, Trans. AIME 180, 430 (1949). Smigelskas, A. D. and Kirkendall, E. O., Zinc diffusion in alpha brass, Trans. AIME 171, 130 (1947). Vineyard, G. H., Frequency factors and isotope effects in solid state rate processes, J. Phys. Chem. Solids 3, 121 (1957). Andersson, J.-O. and Ågren, J., Models for numerical treatment of multicomponent diffusion in simple phases, J. Appl. Phys. 72, 1350 (1992). Previous topic Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo Next topic Phase-field modelling"},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"Phase-field modelling","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html","x":"Topics / Microstructure Topic 9 Phase-field modelling Microstructure is made of interfaces: grain boundaries, phase boundaries, solidification fronts. Tracking each one explicitly through merging, splitting and branching is a bookkeeping nightmare. Phase-field models sidestep it by describing the whole microstructure with smooth fields and letting the interfaces take care of themselves. Reading time about 12 min Builds on 4. Thermodynamics and CALPHAD 8. Diffusion and kinetics Leads to — Category Microstructure After reading this page you should be able to: Describe a diffuse interface and how it relates to interface energy and width. Write the Allen–Cahn and Cahn–Hilliard equations and say which describes which kind of process. Explain spinodal decomposition, curvature-driven motion and coarsening from the equations. List the inputs a quantitative phase-field model needs and the numerical constraints on grid and time step."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"1. The diffuse-interface idea","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#idea","x":"In a sharp-interface description, two phases are separated by a surface of zero thickness, whose motion must be computed and tracked as part of the problem. In a phase-field description, an order parameter φ( r , t ) takes one constant value inside each phase (say φ = −1 and φ = +1) and varies smoothly across a thin transition layer. The interface is wherever φ passes through its mid-value, found after the fact. The motion follows from the equations without special rules, and topology changes (two grains merging, a particle splitting, dendrite arms branching) need no extra treatment. Two types of field appear: Non-conserved order parameters, such as phase identity or crystal orientation, can change locally without the quantity being transported from elsewhere (a region of liquid can freeze in place). Conserved fields, such as composition, can change only by diffusion: material that arrives at one place must leave from another. Many materials problems combine both, such as solidification of an alloy (a phase field plus a concentration field)."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"2. The free-energy functional","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#functional","x":"The model is built on the total free energy of the microstructure as a functional of the fields: F[φ, c] = ∫ V [ f(φ, c, T) + (κ φ /2) |∇φ|² + (κ c /2) |∇c|² + f el + … ] dV The bulk term f carries the thermodynamics: for a two-phase system it has a double-well shape with a minimum for each phase, for example f = ( A /4)(φ² − 1)². In alloys it is taken from CALPHAD (see thermodynamics ). The gradient terms penalise sharp changes, so they set the cost of an interface: without them the field would jump discontinuously to whichever well is lower at each point. The competition between the double well (which wants φ at a minimum) and the gradient term (which wants φ uniform) gives a finite-width interface. For the double well above the equilibrium one-dimensional profile solves κφ″ = f ′(φ), and the solution can be written exactly: φ(x) = tanh( x / ℓ ), ℓ = √(2κ/A), γ = (2√2 / 3) √(κA) The parameters κ and A are thus tied to two physical quantities: the interface energy γ and an interface width (about 2ℓ from the 10–90% points of the profile). Setting them to reproduce a chosen γ and width is the first step in building a model. Additional terms add other physics: elastic energy for misfitting precipitates, magnetic or electric energy, anisotropy in κφ (which yields crystal facets and dendrite arms)."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"3. Allen–Cahn: non-conserved fields","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#ac","x":"The simplest dynamics is relaxation toward lower free energy at a rate proportional to the driving force: ∂φ/∂t = − L δF/δφ = L [ κ ∇²φ − f′(φ) ] with L a kinetic coefficient (interface mobility). This is the Allen–Cahn equation (originally for antiphase boundaries). Two consequences follow. The free energy never increases, dF / dt ≤ 0, so the system moves downhill. And an interface moves with normal velocity proportional to its mean curvature K , toward its centre of curvature: v = − L κ K → for a circle of radius R in two dimensions: d(R²)/dt = −2 L κ so the area of a circular grain shrinks linearly with time, and the smaller of two circles vanishes first, the essential mechanism of normal grain growth. The animation below solves the equation for two circular regions of different size: curved boundaries retreat, the small circle disappears first, and the large one shrinks more slowly because its curvature is lower. Allen–Cahn dynamics in two dimensions (a real numerical solution). The boundary of each disc moves toward its centre of curvature at a speed proportional to its curvature. For many grains, one uses a set of order parameters φ 1 , …, φ n , one per orientation, coupled so that at most one is large at any point (the Fan–Chen model for grain growth) or constrained to sum to one (multi-phase-field models). Misorientation-dependent mobility and energy enter through the coefficients."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"4. Cahn–Hilliard: conserved fields","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#ch","x":"For a conserved quantity such as composition, the flux is driven by the gradient of the chemical potential μ = δ F /δ c (the thermodynamic driving force from the diffusion page), and the equation is a continuity equation: ∂c/∂t = ∇ · ( M ∇μ ), μ = f′(c) − κ ∇²c This is the Cahn–Hilliard equation. It is fourth-order in space (the ∇² acts on μ, which already contains ∇² c ), conserves the total amount of solute, and also lowers the free energy. Spinodal decomposition Suppose a homogeneous alloy with c = c 0 sits inside the spinodal, where f ″( c 0 ) < 0 (see the Gibbs energy curve ). Linearise the equation for a small fluctuation of wavenumber k : it grows as exp[ R ( k ) t ] with R(k) = − M k² ( f″(c 0 ) + κ k² ) For f ″ < 0 this is positive in a band of wavenumbers ( k < √(− f ″/κ)): long-wavelength fluctuations are slow because material must travel far, and short-wavelength ones are suppressed by the interface-energy cost. In between there is a fastest-growing wavenumber, k m = √(− f ″/2κ), which sets the characteristic spacing of the early microstructure, λ m = 2π/ k m . No nucleation barrier is involved; any random noise seeds the pattern. After the compositions reach the two equilibrium values, the structure coarsens : small domains dissolve and large ones grow to lower the total interface area, with a mean domain size increasing as t 1/3 (diffusion-limited coarsening). Cahn–Hilliard spinodal decomposition in two dimensions (a real numerical solution of the equation, periodic boundaries). A nearly uniform mixture, seeded with small random noise, develops composition waves, forms droplets of the minority phase and then coarsens. The mixture here is off-critical, which gives droplets; a 50:50 mixture would give an interconnected pattern."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"5. Models for materials","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#models","x":"Process Typical model Key point Solidification of alloys Wheeler–Boettinger–McFadden (WBM); Kim–Kim–Suzuki (KKS); thin-interface models with an anti-trapping current (Karma) KKS separates interface and bulk contributions by assuming equal chemical potentials inside the interface; the thin-interface limit allows an interface much wider than physical while still giving correct kinetics Grain growth and recrystallisation Fan–Chen multiple order parameters; multi-phase-field (Steinbach) Boundary energy and mobility depend on misorientation; stored energy drives recrystallisation Precipitation and spinodal decomposition Cahn–Hilliard with elastic energy (Khachaturyan, Chen) Misfit strain selects shapes and alignment of precipitates Diffusional and displacive solid-state transformations Concentration field plus phase fields; martensite models with transformation strain Variants and habit planes from elastic energy Fracture Variational approach (Bourdin–Francfort–Marigo; Miehe) A damage field φ ∈ [0,1] degrades stiffness; regularised crack surface energy Domain structures in ferroelectrics and ferromagnets Time-dependent Ginzburg–Landau equations Polarisation or magnetisation as order parameter Corrosion, electrochemistry, sintering Coupled phase-field with transport Moving boundaries and multiple physics"},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"6. Where parameters come from","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#parameters","x":"A phase-field model is only quantitative if its inputs are. The ingredients and their usual sources: Bulk free energy: CALPHAD databases (compositions, driving forces, chemical potentials), with DFT for compounds not covered. Interface energies and their anisotropy: measured (dihedral angles, equilibrium shapes) or computed by atomistic simulation (see molecular dynamics and DFT ). Mobilities: diffusion mobilities from databases (see diffusion ); interface mobilities from experiments (grain growth, recrystallisation) or from MD. Elastic constants and misfit strains: from DFT, lattice parameters and experiments. Interface width: a numerical choice. Physical interfaces are of nanometre width, but resolving them across a micrometre-scale microstructure is prohibitive; the width is therefore chosen as large as the microstructure features allow, and the model is built (or the thin-interface analysis applied) so that results do not depend on it. Check that results are independent of the interface width and the grid spacing before drawing conclusions."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"7. Numerics","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#numerics","x":"Resolution. The interface must be resolved by roughly 4 to 8 grid points across its width. With a coarser grid the interface becomes artificially pinned to the grid and moves in jumps. Explicit time stepping is simple but severely limited: for Allen–Cahn the time step must satisfy roughly Δ t < Δ x ²/(4 L κ) in two dimensions, and for Cahn–Hilliard roughly Δ t < Δ x &sup4;/(32 M κ) because of the fourth-order term, so halving the grid spacing costs sixteen times as many steps. Semi-implicit spectral methods treat the linear gradient terms implicitly in Fourier space and the nonlinear bulk term explicitly. They are fast on periodic domains and allow much larger steps (Chen and Shen, 1998); the animation above uses this approach. Finite elements handle complex geometry and adaptive mesh refinement, which concentrates resolution at interfaces and can reduce cost by orders of magnitude in three dimensions. Large 3D simulations rely on parallel computing, GPUs and adaptive grids. Open-source frameworks include MOOSE (finite elements, with a phase-field module), PRISMS-PF, FiPy, OpenPhase and FEniCS-based codes; MICRESS and Pace3D are commercial and research multi-phase-field programs respectively."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"8. A short program","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#code","x":"The following solves the Cahn–Hilliard equation on a periodic square with the semi-implicit Fourier scheme (non-dimensional units, f = ( c ² − 1)²/4, M = 1, grid spacing 1). Plot c every few hundred steps to reproduce the animation above. import numpy as np N, dt, kappa = 128, 0.2, 1.0 c = -0.25 + 0.03 * np.random.randn(N, N) # off-critical mixture plus noise k = 2 * np.pi * np.fft.fftfreq(N) k2 = k[:, None]**2 + k[None, :]**2 for step in range(5000): f_hat = np.fft.fft2(c**3 - c) # f'(c) for the double well c = np.real(np.fft.ifft2((np.fft.fft2(c) - dt * k2 * f_hat) / (1 + dt * kappa * k2**2))) print(\"mean c is conserved:\", c.mean()) Change the initial mean to 0 to get the interconnected pattern; change κ to alter the domain size; and check that the average composition stays constant, a property of the conservative equation."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"9. Strengths and limits","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#limits","x":"Strengths. No front tracking; arbitrary topology; the free-energy structure makes it easy to couple thermodynamics, elasticity, electromagnetism and other physics; results are visual, with morphology directly comparable with micrographs. Limits. Cost grows quickly with domain size and with the need to resolve the interface; quantitative accuracy depends on how well the interface energy, mobility and (especially) anisotropy are known; many parameters are poorly known and are calibrated; the diffuse interface can create artefacts (solute trapping, spurious interface effects) unless the model is formulated carefully; and the result is a continuum description, so atomic-scale features (dislocation cores, interface structure) must be supplied from below. Three dimensions and long times are expensive. Phase-field is best used with a clear question about morphology or kinetics, in a regime where the microstructure scale (tens of nanometres to millimetres) is what matters. Key takeaways Interfaces are smooth transitions of an order parameter, so topology changes need no special treatment. Allen-Cahn (non-conserved) gives curvature-driven motion; Cahn-Hilliard (conserved) gives spinodal decomposition and t to the 1/3 coarsening. Interface energy and width fix the model parameters: gamma = (2 sqrt 2 / 3) sqrt(kappa A) and width ~ sqrt(2 kappa / A). Inputs come from CALPHAD (free energy), diffusion databases (mobility) and atomistics (interface energy); results must not depend on the interface width or the grid. Resolve the interface with about 4 to 8 grid points; explicit steps are tightly limited, so use semi-implicit spectral or adaptive finite elements."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#check","x":"For the double well f = ( A /4)(φ² − 1)², what happens to the interface width and interface energy if κ is quadrupled? The width ℓ = √(2κ/ A ) doubles, and the interface energy γ = (2√2/3)√(κ A ) also doubles. Widths and energies cannot be set independently without changing both κ and A . A Cahn–Hilliard run with a time step typical for Allen–Cahn becomes unstable. Why? The Cahn–Hilliard equation has a fourth-order spatial operator, so the explicit stability limit scales as Δ x 4 instead of Δ x ². The same step that is safe for Allen–Cahn can be far too large. Use a smaller step or a semi-implicit scheme. Why does a mixture inside the spinodal decompose with a characteristic length scale, instead of at the largest wavelength? Growth rate R ( k ) is zero at k = 0 (no net transport is possible) and negative above a cut-off set by the interface cost, so it has a maximum at an intermediate wavenumber k m . That mode grows fastest and dominates the early microstructure."},{"n":9,"t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#refs","x":"Provatas, N. and Elder, K., Phase-Field Methods in Materials Science and Engineering , Wiley-VCH (2010). Chen, L.-Q., Phase-field models for microstructure evolution, Annu. Rev. Mater. Res. 32, 113 (2002). Moelans, N., Blanpain, B. and Wollants, P., An introduction to phase-field modeling of microstructure evolution, Calphad 32, 268 (2008). Steinbach, I., Phase-field models in materials science, Modelling Simul. Mater. Sci. Eng. 17, 073001 (2009). Cahn, J. W. and Hilliard, J. E., J. Chem. Phys. 28, 258 (1958); Allen, S. M. and Cahn, J. W., Acta Metall. 27, 1085 (1979). Wheeler, A. A., Boettinger, W. J. and McFadden, G. B., Phase-field model for isothermal phase transitions in binary alloys, Phys. Rev. A 45, 7424 (1992). Kim, S. G., Kim, W. T. and Suzuki, T., Phase-field model for binary alloys, Phys. Rev. E 60, 7186 (1999). Karma, A. and Rappel, W.-J., Quantitative phase-field modeling of dendritic growth in two and three dimensions, Phys. Rev. E 57, 4323 (1998). Steinbach, I. et al., A phase field concept for multiphase systems, Physica D 94, 135 (1996). Fan, D. and Chen, L.-Q., Computer simulation of grain growth using a continuum field model, Acta Mater. 45, 611 (1997). Chen, L. Q. and Shen, J., Applications of semi-implicit Fourier-spectral method to phase field equations, Comput. Phys. Commun. 108, 147 (1998). Previous topic Diffusion and kinetics Next topic The finite element method"},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"The finite element method","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html","x":"Topics / Continuum Topic 10 The finite element method At the scale of components and processes, a material is treated as a continuum and its behaviour is governed by partial differential equations for heat, stress and flow. The finite element method (FEM) is the standard way to solve them on real geometries, and it is where the microstructure and properties computed at finer scales finally meet engineering use. Reading time about 13 min Builds on 8. Diffusion and kinetics Leads to — Category Continuum After reading this page you should be able to: State the governing equations of thermal and mechanical continuum problems. Explain the weak form, shape functions and the assembly of the stiffness matrix. Perform and interpret a mesh-convergence study. Describe how nonlinear, transient and coupled problems are solved and what inputs a material model needs."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"1. Governing equations","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#equations","x":"Continuum models express conservation laws together with a description of the material (the constitutive law ). The most common in materials work are: Physics Equation Unknown Heat conduction ρc p ∂T/∂t = ∇·(k∇T) + q Temperature T Mechanical equilibrium (static) ∇·σ + b = 0, ε = ½(∇u + ∇u T ) Displacement u Linear elasticity (isotropic) σ = λ tr(ε) I + 2μ ε Stress σ from strain ε Fluid flow (incompressible) ρ(∂v/∂t + v·∇v) = −∇p + μ∇²v + f, ∇·v = 0 Velocity v, pressure p Species transport ∂c/∂t + v·∇c = ∇·(D∇c) + r Concentration c Here σ is the Cauchy stress, b a body force, λ and μ the Lamé constants (μ is the shear modulus), k the thermal conductivity, q a heat source and ρ the density. Real problems couple these: heat generated by plastic work, thermal strain from temperature gradients, phase transformations that release latent heat and change volume."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"2. From strong to weak form","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#weak","x":"The equations above, the strong form , require derivatives that may not exist in real problems (a stress jump at an interface between two materials, a point load). The FEM starts from an equivalent weak form . Multiply the equilibrium equation by an arbitrary test function v (a virtual displacement), integrate over the body, and use integration by parts to move one derivative onto v . For linear elasticity: ∫ Ω ε(v) : C : ε(u) dV = ∫ Ω v · b dV + ∫ Γ t v · t dS for all admissible v This is the principle of virtual work: internal virtual work equals external. It needs only first derivatives, handles discontinuous material properties naturally, and has the boundary traction t built in, so natural boundary conditions (applied forces, fluxes) appear automatically, while essential boundary conditions (prescribed displacement or temperature) must be imposed on the trial function."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"3. Discretisation","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#discretise","x":"The domain is divided into small elements (triangles or quadrilaterals in 2D; tetrahedra, hexahedra or prisms in 3D) joined at nodes . Within each element the unknown is approximated by simple shape functions N i ( x ) that equal 1 at node i and 0 at the other nodes, so the nodal values are the unknowns: u(x) ≈ Σ i N i (x) u i Choosing the test functions from the same space (the Galerkin method) turns the weak form into a linear system for the nodal values: K u = f, K ij = ∫ B i T C B j dV, f i = ∫ N i b dV + ∫ N i t dS where B contains derivatives of the shape functions (it turns nodal displacements into strains). The stiffness matrix K is built element by element and assembled into the global matrix, with integrals evaluated by Gauss quadrature on a reference element and mapped to the real shape (isoparametric elements). It is sparse, symmetric and positive definite for stable problems, and is solved by direct sparse solvers for moderate sizes or iterative solvers (conjugate gradients with preconditioning, multigrid) for large ones. After imposing the essential boundary conditions, solve for u ; strains and stresses follow by differentiation within each element. Element choice: linear elements represent displacement piecewise linearly (so strain is piecewise constant); quadratic elements use mid-side nodes and represent curved variation far better for the same number of elements. Higher-order elements converge faster in smooth regions; low-order elements are more robust for contact and large deformation."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"4. A worked example","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#example","x":"An elastic bar of length L , cross-section area A and modulus E is clamped at the left end and loaded by a force P at the right end. Use two equal linear elements of length h = L /2 with three nodes. For a two-node element the element stiffness matrix is k e = (EA / h) [ [1, −1], [−1, 1] ] Assembling the two elements gives a 3×3 matrix. With node 1 fixed ( u 1 = 0), the reduced system for nodes 2 and 3 is (EA / h) [ [2, −1], [−1, 1] ] [ u 2 , u 3 ] T = [ 0, P ] T Solving gives u 2 = Ph /( EA ) and u 3 = 2 Ph /( EA ) = PL /( EA ), which is the exact analytical result for tip displacement. (In one dimension the nodal values are exact for such problems; in 2D and 3D, they are not.) The strain in each element is the difference of nodal displacements divided by h , giving constant strain P /( EA ) and stress P / A everywhere, again exact."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"5. Meshes and convergence","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#mesh","x":"FEM solutions approximate the true solution, and the error depends on how fine and good the mesh is. For a sufficiently smooth solution and element polynomial degree p , the error in displacement falls as h p +1 and the error in strain and stress (the energy norm) as h p , where h is the element size. For linear elements, halving h cuts the displacement error by about four. Refining the mesh. The piecewise-linear approximation of u(x) = sin(πx) with 2, 4, 8 and 16 elements; the stated maximum error falls by a factor of about four each time the elements are halved. Mesh convergence study A result is trustworthy only if it stops changing when the mesh is refined. The procedure: solve on at least three meshes with a systematic refinement ratio, tabulate the quantity of interest (peak stress, displacement, temperature, reaction force), and check that it converges. Richardson extrapolation of the three values estimates both the converged value and the discretisation error. Mesh quality and refinement Refine where gradients are steep : around holes, notches, contact zones, welds, interfaces, crack tips. Adaptive refinement does this automatically from an error estimate. Element shape matters. Avoid very elongated or distorted elements; check aspect ratio, skewness and Jacobian. Stress singularities. At a sharp re-entrant corner or crack tip the exact stress is infinite, and refinement makes the computed peak stress grow without converging. Use a fillet of realistic radius, special crack-tip elements, or a quantity that is finite (such as the J -integral) instead of the peak stress. Locking. Low-order elements can be far too stiff when the physics constrains deformation: nearly incompressible behaviour (plasticity, rubber) causes volumetric locking ; thin bending-dominated structures cause shear locking . Remedies are reduced integration (with hourglass control), selective or “B-bar” integration, mixed formulations and higher-order elements. A useful analytical check is the Kirsch problem: a small circular hole in a wide plate under uniaxial tension has a peak stress of three times the applied stress, at the edge of the hole. A model that does not recover a factor near 3 on a fine mesh has a set-up error."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"6. Nonlinear and transient problems","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#nonlinear","x":"Most interesting problems are nonlinear: material nonlinearity (plasticity, creep, temperature-dependent properties), geometric nonlinearity (large rotations and strains), and contact (boundary conditions that change with the solution). The equations become R ( u ) = f ext − f int ( u ) = 0 and are solved iteratively by Newton–Raphson : linearise with the tangent stiffness K T = ∂ f int /∂ u , solve for the correction, update and repeat until the residual is small. The load is applied in increments so that each Newton solve starts close to the solution, and line searches or arc-length methods help with softening and snap-through. For time-dependent problems the semi-discrete equations have the form M d T /d t + K T = f (thermal) or M a + C v + K u = f (dynamic, with v and a the nodal velocity and acceleration), and are advanced in time by a scheme analogous to those in the diffusion page. Explicit schemes (central difference) have no equation solve per step but are conditionally stable, with step limited by the time a wave takes to cross the smallest element (roughly h / c , with c the wave speed); they suit high-speed events and forming with complicated contact. Implicit schemes (backward Euler, Newmark) are stable for larger steps but require a nonlinear solve each step; they suit slow, quasi-static and thermal problems."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"7. Constitutive models","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#materials","x":"The model is only as good as the constitutive law, which is where the lower-scale methods feed in. Typical choices for metals: Elastic constants ( E , ν, or the full tensor for anisotropic crystals), temperature-dependent. Plasticity : a yield criterion (von Mises for isotropic metals, Hill or Barlat for sheet anisotropy), a flow rule and a hardening law. Common hardening forms are the Hollomon power law σ = K ε n , the Voce saturation law and the Johnson–Cook model with strain-rate and temperature terms. Viscoplasticity and creep : Norton power law ε̇ = A σ n exp(− Q / RT ), with the Arrhenius dependence linking to diffusion . Crystal plasticity : grain-level slip with orientation, giving texture and anisotropy from microstructure (Section 8). Thermo-physical data as functions of temperature: conductivity, specific heat (including latent heat), density, thermal expansion; and boundary conditions such as heat-transfer coefficients and friction coefficients, which are frequently the largest uncertainty in process models. Parameters are calibrated against tests (tension and compression across temperature and rate, relaxation, creep). Fit the model to some experiments and check it against others not used for the fit."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"8. Applications in materials","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#applications","x":"Application What is modelled Notes Casting and continuous casting Heat flow with latent heat release, fluid flow in the melt, shrinkage, stresses The enthalpy method handles latent heat; coupling with CALPHAD gives the solid-fraction curve Welding Moving heat source (for example the Goldak double-ellipsoid), distortion and residual stress, phase transformation in the heat-affected zone Residual stress depends strongly on transformation strains Heat treatment and quenching Thermal–metallurgical–mechanical coupling: transformation kinetics, volume change and transformation plasticity Distortion and cracking risk; cooling-rate dependent microstructure Hot and cold rolling, forging, extrusion Large plastic deformation, contact, friction, temperature Often explicit or implicit with remeshing; flow stress from physically based models Sheet forming and springback Anisotropic plasticity, bending, contact Yield criterion and hardening at large strain control accuracy; shear-locking-free elements Fatigue and fracture Stress concentrations, crack-tip fields, J -integral, cohesive-zone and extended FEM crack growth Mesh around the crack tip determines accuracy Crystal-plasticity FEM (CPFEM) Grain-level slip in a polycrystal or a representative volume element (RVE), predicting texture, anisotropy and local strain Spectral-solver alternatives (for example DAMASK) are common for periodic RVEs Homogenisation Effective properties of composites and multiphase steels from an RVE under periodic boundary conditions Results must lie between the Voigt (uniform strain) and Reuss (uniform stress) bounds The Voigt and Reuss bounds for the effective modulus of a mixture with volume fractions f i are E V = Σ f i E i and 1/ E R = Σ f i / E i ; any computed effective modulus outside this range indicates an error."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"9. Verification and validation","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#quality","x":"Verification: check against analytical solutions (bars, beams, the Kirsch plate, one-dimensional transient conduction), run the patch test (a mesh must reproduce uniform strain exactly), use manufactured solutions , and perform mesh and time-step convergence studies. Sanity checks: reaction forces equal applied loads; energy balances; symmetry is preserved; units are consistent (FEM codes are unit-less, so any consistent system works, but a mix of mm and Pa does not). Validation: compare with measurements not used in calibration, such as thermocouple histories, strain fields from digital image correlation, residual stress by diffraction, and measured distortion. Sensitivity: vary uncertain inputs (friction, heat-transfer coefficient, flow-curve parameters) to see which ones control the answer."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"10. Software","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#software","x":"Commercial: Abaqus, Ansys, COMSOL Multiphysics, MSC Marc, LS-DYNA (explicit); process-specific codes such as DEFORM (forming and heat treatment), Simufact, ProCAST and MAGMA (casting), SYSWELD (welding and heat treatment). Open source: CalculiX, Code_Aster, Elmer, FEniCSx, deal.II, MOOSE, FreeFEM; Gmsh for meshing and ParaView for visualisation; OpenFOAM for finite-volume fluid and heat transfer problems."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"11. A short program","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#code","x":"A complete finite element solution of a one-dimensional bar under uniform body force, in plain Python, shows assembly and boundary conditions. The problem is − u ″ = 1 on (0, L ) with u (0) = 0 and zero force at x = L , whose exact solution is u = Lx − x ²/2: import numpy as np L, n = 1.0, 8 # bar length, number of elements x = np.linspace(0, L, n + 1) K = np.zeros((n + 1, n + 1)) f = np.zeros(n + 1) for e in range(n): # assemble element by element h = x[e + 1] - x[e] K[e:e + 2, e:e + 2] += (1.0 / h) * np.array([[1, -1], [-1, 1]]) # EA = 1 f[e:e + 2] += h / 2 * 1.0 # uniform body force of 1 u = np.zeros(n + 1) u[1:] = np.linalg.solve(K[1:, 1:], f[1:]) # u(0) = 0 imposed by removing node 0 exact = x * (L - x / 2) print(\"max nodal error:\", np.max(np.abs(u - exact))) The nodal error is at the level of rounding error: for this equation, linear elements in one dimension give exact nodal values. Change the load or the coefficient (for example a stiffness that varies with x ) and the nodal values stop being exact; then compare the error for n = 4, 8, 16 to see the convergence rate directly. Key takeaways The weak form with shape functions turns a PDE into a sparse linear system, K u = f, assembled element by element. A mesh convergence study is mandatory. Displacement error falls as h to the p+1 for smooth solutions, while stress singularities never converge. Nonlinear problems use Newton-Raphson with load steps; time-dependent ones use explicit or implicit integration. The constitutive law and boundary conditions (heat transfer, friction) usually dominate the uncertainty. Verify against analytic cases (the Kirsch plate gives a factor of 3, the patch test) and validate against measurements."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"12. Check your understanding","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#check","x":"Why is the peak stress at a sharp internal corner not a reliable result? The exact elastic solution has a singularity there, so the computed peak stress increases without limit as the mesh is refined. A converged answer requires a physically realistic corner radius, or a different measure such as a stress intensity factor or J -integral. A transient thermal model of a quench gives implausible results. Name three inputs to check. The heat-transfer coefficient at the surface (it varies strongly with temperature and boiling regime), the temperature-dependent specific heat and latent heat of transformation, and the initial temperature distribution. Then check time-step size and mesh density near the surface where gradients are steepest. Your explicit dynamics run becomes unstable after you refine the mesh. Why? The stable time step is bounded by the time a stress wave takes to cross the smallest element, about h / c . Halving the element size halves the stable step, and a time step fixed from the coarse mesh now exceeds the limit."},{"n":10,"t":"The finite element method","h":"13. References and further reading","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#refs","x":"Zienkiewicz, O. C., Taylor, R. L. and Zhu, J. Z., The Finite Element Method: Its Basis and Fundamentals , Butterworth-Heinemann. Hughes, T. J. R., The Finite Element Method: Linear Static and Dynamic Finite Element Analysis , Dover. Bathe, K.-J., Finite Element Procedures , Prentice Hall. Belytschko, T., Liu, W. K., Moran, B. and Elkhodary, K. I., Nonlinear Finite Elements for Continua and Structures , Wiley. Cook, R. D., Malkus, D. S., Plesha, M. E. and Witt, R. J., Concepts and Applications of Finite Element Analysis , Wiley. Goldak, J., Chakravarti, A. and Bibby, M., A new finite element model for welding heat sources, Metall. Trans. B 15, 299 (1984). Roters, F. et al., Overview of constitutive laws, kinematics, homogenization and multiscale methods in crystal plasticity finite-element modeling, Acta Mater. 58, 1152 (2010). Roters, F. et al., DAMASK: the Düsseldorf Advanced Material Simulation Kit, Comput. Mater. Sci. 158, 420 (2019). Previous topic Phase-field modelling Next topic Machine learning for materials"},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"Machine learning for materials","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html","x":"Topics / Data Topic 11 Machine learning for materials Machine learning (ML) fits flexible functions to data. In materials science it supplies fast surrogates for expensive simulations, extracts structure–property relationships from experiments, and guides which calculation or experiment to do next. It interpolates well and extrapolates badly, and most of the craft lies in knowing the difference. Reading time about 15 min Builds on 5. Density functional theory 6. Molecular dynamics Leads to — Category Data After reading this page you should be able to: Describe the main uses of ML in materials and the workflow for a supervised model. Choose descriptors and a model suited to the amount and kind of data. Validate a model honestly, including with grouped cross-validation and baselines. Explain how machine-learned interatomic potentials and active learning work, and recognise the common pitfalls."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"1. Where machine learning fits","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#role","x":"The methods in the earlier topics are built from physical laws, which give reliable predictions but at a computational cost that limits how many materials, structures or conditions can be examined. ML trades some of that generality for speed: it learns a mapping from inputs to outputs from examples, then evaluates it in microseconds. The main uses in materials are: Property prediction. Predict a property (formation energy, band gap, strength, hardness, fatigue life) from composition, structure or processing history. Interatomic potentials. Reproduce DFT energies and forces for molecular dynamics at a fraction of the cost (Section 8). Surrogate models. Replace a slow simulation (a finite-element model, a phase-field run) by a fast approximation to allow optimisation or uncertainty analysis. Inverse design and discovery. Search a large space of compositions or processes for ones meeting a target, with the model proposing the next candidate (Section 9). Characterisation. Segment and classify micrographs, identify phases from diffraction patterns, analyse spectra, extract data from the literature. All of these rely on the premise that similar inputs give similar outputs. They work where the data cover the question and fail where they do not. The “learning” is statistics, not understanding, so ML is best treated as one more tool beside the physical models, and its predictions as hypotheses to test."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"2. The workflow","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#workflow","x":"Define the target and the decision it informs: what accuracy is useful, and what error would be harmful? Collect and clean data (Section 3), documenting its origin. Featurise : convert each material or process into numbers (Section 4). Split the data into training, validation and test sets in a way that mimics the intended use (Section 6). Train and tune models, starting with simple ones and a trivial baseline. Validate on the held-out test set; examine errors, not only averages; quantify uncertainty. Deploy and monitor : check predictions against new measurements and retrain as data grow. Supervised learning (input–output examples) covers regression (continuous property) and classification (phase present or not, stable or unstable). Unsupervised learning finds structure without labels: clustering of microstructures, dimensionality reduction (PCA, t-SNE, UMAP) to visualise a composition space or a set of atomic environments."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"3. Data","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#data","x":"Data are almost always the limiting factor. Computed data are abundant and consistent (Materials Project, OQMD, AFLOW, JARVIS, NOMAD; see the introduction ), with hundreds of thousands of DFT entries; experimental data are scarcer, noisier and heterogeneous, with typical industrial datasets of tens to thousands of samples. Small data. A few hundred points constrain a model to be simple; complex models memorise. Use strong prior information (physics-based features), simple models and cross-validation. Bias. Published and tested materials are those that worked. A model trained only on successes cannot learn what fails; record negative results. Heterogeneity. The same nominal property measured in different laboratories, specimens or conditions differs. Record the conditions (temperature, strain rate, grain size, heat treatment) as features or normalise carefully. Quality. Remove duplicates, check units and outliers, and keep a record of every cleaning step. Errors in the training data are learned. Provenance. Store raw data, code and software versions so that results can be reproduced and data reused (FAIR principles: findable, accessible, interoperable, reusable)."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"4. Descriptors","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#features","x":"A model needs each material as a fixed-length vector of numbers, the descriptor or feature vector. Good descriptors encode the physics that matters; they are the main way prior knowledge enters. Descriptor type Examples When to use Composition-based Statistics (mean, range, deviation) of elemental properties such as radius, electronegativity, valence electrons, melting point (the “Magpie” set) When the structure is unknown, for example in alloy and glass composition screening Structure-based Radial and angular distribution functions, Coulomb matrix, smooth overlap of atomic positions (SOAP), symmetry functions, atomic cluster expansion When atomic positions are given; essential for potentials and structure–property maps Graph-based (learned) Crystal graphs where atoms are nodes and bonds are edges; message-passing and equivariant neural networks learn the features Large datasets of structures Process and microstructure Composition, heating and cooling temperatures, times and rates, rolling reduction, grain size, phase fractions Experimental property prediction for alloys and processes Physics-derived Quantities from CALPHAD or DFT, such as phase fractions, driving forces, stacking-fault energy, diffusivity When the output is governed by thermodynamics or kinetics that a simulation can estimate The libraries matminer, pymatgen and DScribe compute many of these. Two rules help: features must be invariant to things that should not matter (atom labelling, rotation, translation, choice of unit cell), and the number of features should be small compared with the number of samples, or regularisation must control it."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"5. Models","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#models","x":"Model Suited to Strengths Weaknesses Linear, ridge, LASSO Tens to thousands of samples Simple, interpretable; LASSO selects a sparse set of features Cannot capture strong nonlinearity Gaussian process, kernel ridge Small to medium data Smooth interpolation; Gaussian process gives uncertainty; basis of several interatomic potentials Cost grows as N 3 with training set size (approximations exist) Random forest, gradient-boosted trees Tabular data of hundreds to hundreds of thousands Robust, little tuning, handle mixed features, give importances Cannot extrapolate beyond the training range; piecewise-constant predictions Neural networks (fully connected) Large data Flexible; scales to millions Needs more data and tuning; harder uncertainty Graph and equivariant networks Large structural datasets, potentials Learn descriptors; built-in symmetries give data efficiency Costly to train; need care with out-of-distribution structures Symbolic regression and sparse models (for example SISSO) Small data with physical intuition Produce compact formulas that can be inspected Search is expensive; results sensitive to the candidate features A sensible rule is to begin with the simplest model that could work and a trivial baseline (always predicting the mean, or a physical rule of thumb), and to add complexity only while the validation error keeps improving."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"6. Validation","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#validation","x":"The goal of a model is accuracy on new cases, not on the data it was trained on. A flexible model can fit its training data perfectly and still predict poorly, as the animation shows: a straight line is too simple ( underfitting , high bias), a moderate polynomial captures the trend, and a high-degree polynomial threads through every noisy point but oscillates wildly in between ( overfitting , high variance). Fits of increasing flexibility to the same twelve noisy points. The error on the training points keeps falling as the degree rises, but the error against the true underlying curve is smallest for the moderate model and explodes for the most flexible one. Splits and cross-validation Reserve a test set that is never used for fitting or tuning, and use k-fold cross-validation on the rest to choose models and hyperparameters. Materials data need extra care because samples are not independent: many entries in a database are small variations of the same chemistry, so a random split puts near-duplicates in both training and test sets and gives optimistic scores. Better splits for materials are: Grouped splits : all members of a chemical family, composition system or alloy heat stay together (leave-one-cluster-out). Time-based splits : train on older experiments, test on newer ones, as the model will actually be used. Extrapolation tests : hold out the highest or lowest values of the target, or new chemical systems, and measure the degradation in accuracy. Metrics and diagnostics Report mean absolute error (MAE, in the units of the property), RMSE and R 2 , and always compare with a baseline model; a parity plot (predicted against measured) shows where the errors are; a learning curve (error against training-set size) shows whether more data would help or whether the model or descriptors are the limit. Absolute accuracy matters less than accuracy relative to the spread in the data and to the difference you need to resolve."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"7. Uncertainty","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#uncertainty","x":"A prediction without an uncertainty is hard to act on. Two kinds exist: aleatoric (noise in the data, irreducible) and epistemic (lack of data in this region, reducible). Methods include the predictive variance of Gaussian processes, the spread of an ensemble of models trained on resampled data or with different initialisations, dropout-based estimates, and conformal prediction , which gives intervals with a guaranteed coverage under mild assumptions. Check calibration : among predictions with 90% intervals, about 90% of true values should fall inside. The applicability domain , the region of descriptor space where training data exist, should be part of the model: a prediction far from all training points is a guess, whatever the number looks like."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"8. Machine-learned interatomic potentials","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#potentials","x":"The most successful use of ML in atomistic modelling is the machine-learned interatomic potential (MLIP). It writes the energy of a configuration as a sum of atomic contributions, each a learned function of the neighbourhood of an atom within a cutoff, and trains on DFT energies, forces and stresses: E = Σ i ε( descriptor of the environment of atom i ), F i = −∇ i E Because forces come from differentiating a single energy function, energy is conserved. The function must be invariant to translation, rotation and swapping identical atoms, which is built into the descriptors (SOAP, symmetry functions, atomic cluster expansion) or into the architecture (equivariant message-passing networks such as NequIP and MACE). Families include the Behler–Parrinello neural-network potentials, Gaussian approximation potentials, moment tensor potentials, SNAP, and equivariant graph networks. Classical (EAM, Tersoff) Machine-learned DFT Cost per atom per step ∼ 1 µs ∼ 10 µs to 10 ms (depends on model) ∼ 1 to 1000 s Accuracy versus DFT (typical) Tens to hundreds of meV/atom for complex properties Few meV/atom in energy; 20–100 meV/Å in forces Reference Transferability Limited by the functional form Good inside training data; poor outside General Effort to build Months of parameter fitting by experts Generating training data and training None Building one properly Training data must span what the simulation will visit: perfect crystals, strained cells, defects, surfaces, liquid configurations, high-temperature MD snapshots, and saddle points if barriers matter. Active learning : run MD with the current potential, flag configurations where an ensemble of potentials disagrees (high uncertainty), label them with DFT, retrain, and repeat. This is how a potential becomes robust without enormous databases. Validate on properties, not only on a test set of energies. A low error on held-out DFT frames does not guarantee a correct lattice constant, elastic constants, phonons, defect energies, stacking-fault energies or melting point. Check those, as for any interatomic potential . Watch for the loss of physics at short range (atoms colliding) and long range (electrostatics, dispersion), which are not captured by a finite cutoff unless added explicitly. Universal (foundation) potentials such as M3GNet, CHGNet and MACE-MP-0 were trained on large databases of DFT relaxations covering most of"},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"9. Active learning and design","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#active","x":"When evaluating the true function (an experiment, a DFT calculation) is expensive, ML can decide what to evaluate next . Bayesian optimisation fits a surrogate with uncertainty (typically a Gaussian process) to the data collected so far and picks the next point by maximising an acquisition function that balances exploitation (high predicted value) and exploration (high uncertainty), such as expected improvement or upper confidence bound. It can find good alloys or process settings in tens of experiments where a grid would need thousands. Multi-objective versions search for the Pareto front of trade-offs (for example strength against ductility). Combined with automation, these loops form “self-driving laboratories”. The same logic improves training sets for potentials and surrogates (Section 8). Design is an extrapolation task: the goal is a material better than those in the data. Treat the predictions as candidates to verify by experiment or by a physics-based calculation, not as results."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"10. Combining with physics","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#physics","x":"Physics-based features (thermodynamic phase fractions, driving forces, computed diffusivities) as model inputs improve accuracy on small datasets. Hybrid models let ML correct the error of a physical model (a residual) or supply a term inside it, for example a flow stress inside a finite-element model, so the physical structure keeps the extrapolation sensible. Multi-fidelity and transfer learning : pre-train on abundant cheap data (simulation, lower-accuracy DFT) and fine-tune on scarce accurate data (experiments). Constraints such as monotonic dependence on temperature, positivity, conservation laws or symmetry can be imposed in the architecture or loss. Physics-informed neural networks add the residual of a differential equation to the loss so the network solves the PDE; they are useful for inverse problems with sparse data but are generally slower and less accurate than conventional solvers for forward problems. Interpretation. Feature importances, partial-dependence plots and SHAP values indicate which inputs drive predictions. They show association learned from the data, not causation, and should be compared against known mechanisms."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"11. Common pitfalls","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#pitfalls","x":"Data leakage. Information from the test set influences training: near-duplicates across splits, normalisation computed on all data before splitting, features that are functions of the target. Tuning on the test set. Repeated peeking turns the test set into a validation set; keep a final untouched set. No baseline. A model with R 2 = 0.8 may be worse than a simple rule that gives 0.85. Extrapolation presented as interpolation. High scores on random splits hide failure on new chemistry. Ignoring uncertainty and the applicability domain. Confusing correlation with mechanism , especially in observational process data where variables are correlated by how the plant is run. Irreproducibility. Unrecorded random seeds, software versions or data versions."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"12. A short program","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#code","x":"A random-forest regression validated with grouped cross-validation and compared with a mean-value baseline, using scikit-learn. X , y and groups come from your own dataset: groups gives the chemical family or alloy system of each row so that related samples are never split between training and testing. import numpy as np from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor from sklearn.model_selection import GroupKFold, cross_val_predict from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error # X: (n_samples, n_features) descriptors; y: target property; groups: family id per row model = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=300, random_state=0) pred = cross_val_predict(model, X, y, cv=GroupKFold(n_splits=5), groups=groups) print(\"model MAE :\", mean_absolute_error(y, pred)) print(\"baseline MAE:\", mean_absolute_error(y, np.full_like(y, y.mean()))) Try replacing GroupKFold with ordinary shuffled k-fold and compare: if the error drops sharply, your data contain many near-duplicates, and the grouped score is the honest one. Key takeaways ML interpolates well and extrapolates badly: trust it inside the training domain and doubt it outside. Descriptors carry the physics and symmetry; small datasets favour simple models and physics-based features. Validate with grouped, time-based or extrapolation splits against a baseline, and report uncertainty. Machine-learned potentials reach near-DFT accuracy at far lower cost; build them with active learning and validate on properties, not only on energies. Active learning and Bayesian optimisation choose the next calculation or experiment; combine them with physical models."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"13. Check your understanding","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#check","x":"A model trained on 50 000 database entries reports R 2 = 0.98 with a random 80/20 split. Why might it still fail on a new compound? The database contains many closely related compositions, so a random split puts similar entries in training and test sets, and the score measures interpolation among near-duplicates. A new chemical family lies outside the training distribution. Test with grouped or leave-one-cluster-out splits. Why do machine-learned potentials need uncertainty estimates in active learning? During MD the potential may visit configurations not represented in the training data, where its prediction is unreliable but looks plausible. Disagreement between an ensemble of potentials flags these configurations; computing DFT only for them adds the most informative training data at the least cost. Training error keeps falling but validation error rises. What is happening and what can you do? The model is overfitting. Options: reduce model complexity, add regularisation, increase the amount of data, use better descriptors, stop training earlier, or average an ensemble."},{"n":11,"t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"14. References and further reading","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#refs","x":"Butler, K. T., Davies, D. W., Cartwright, H., Isayev, O. and Walsh, A., Machine learning for molecular and materials science, Nature 559, 547 (2018). Schmidt, J., Marques, M. R. G., Botti, S. and Marques, M. A. L., Recent advances and applications of machine learning in solid-state materials science, npj Comput. Mater. 5, 83 (2019). Hastie, T., Tibshirani, R. and Friedman, J., The Elements of Statistical Learning , Springer. Rasmussen, C. E. and Williams, C. K. I., Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning , MIT Press. Ward, L., Agrawal, A., Choudhary, A. and Wolverton, C., A general-purpose machine learning framework for predicting properties of inorganic materials, npj Comput. Mater. 2, 16028 (2016). Ward, L. et al., Matminer: an open source toolkit for materials data mining, Comput. Mater. Sci. 152, 60 (2018). Meredig, B. et al., Can machine learning identify the next high-temperature superconductor? Examining extrapolation performance for materials discovery, Mol. Syst. Des. Eng. 3, 819 (2018). Behler, J. and Parrinello, M., Phys. Rev. Lett. 98, 146401 (2007); Bartók, A. P. et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 104, 136403 (2010). Bartók, A. P., Kondor, R. and Csányi, G., On representing chemical environments, Phys. Rev. B 87, 184115 (2013). Xie, T. and Grossman, J. C., Crystal graph convolutional neural networks, Phys. Rev. Lett. 120, 145301 (2018). Batzner, S. et al., E(3)-equivariant graph neural networks for data-efficient and accurate interatomic potentials, Nat. Commun. 13, 2453 (2022). Chen, C. and Ong, S. P., A universal graph deep learning interatomic potential for the periodic table, Nat. Comput. Sci. 2, 718 (2022). Lookman, T., Balachandran, P. V., Xue, D. and Yuan, R., Active learning in materials science with emphasis on adaptive sampling using uncertainties for targeted design, npj Comput. Mater. 5, 21 (2019). Deringer, V. L. et al., Gaussian process regression for materials and molecules, Chem. Rev. 121, 10073 (2021). Raissi, M., Perdikaris, P. and Karniadakis, G. E., Physics-informed neural networks, J. Comput. Phys. 378, 686 (2019). Previous topic The finite element method"}];
